// Graph Reasoning passages (original): plotted figures (line/bar, drawn by figureHtml) with questions whose
// worked solutions play step by step: "hl" highlights passage/question text, "graph" traces values on the figure.
const GRAPH_PASSAGES = [
  {"id":"p_graph_biochem_1","subj":"biochemistry","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biochemistry","title":"Coupled and Uncoupled Turnover in p-Hydroxybenzoate Hydroxylase","passage":"p-Hydroxybenzoate hydroxylase (PHBH) is a bacterial flavoprotein monooxygenase that converts p-hydroxybenzoate (pOHB) to 3,4-dihydroxybenzoate. Its catalytic cycle begins when NADPH transfers a hydride to the enzyme-bound FAD, forming FADH⁻ and NADP⁺ (Reaction 1). FADH⁻ then reacts with O₂ to form a C4a-hydroperoxyflavin, FADH–OOH (Reaction 2). If an aromatic substrate is positioned correctly, the distal oxygen of FADH–OOH is transferred to the ring, and the remaining flavin intermediate loses water to regenerate FAD (Reaction 3a). If no hydroxylatable ring is positioned correctly, FADH–OOH instead decomposes to FAD and H₂O₂ (Reaction 3b), a process called uncoupling.\n\nIn the free enzyme, Reaction 1 is extremely slow; it is accelerated more than 10⁴-fold when an aromatic ligand binds, which keeps the enzyme from wasting NADPH. To test how three ligands affect coupling, researchers incubated PHBH with excess NADPH in air-saturated buffer and increasing concentrations of pOHB, p-aminobenzoate (pAB), or 6-hydroxynicotinate (6-HN). They separately measured the rate of hydroxylated product formation by HPLC and the rate of H₂O₂ formation with a peroxidase-coupled assay. Both rates were measured during the first minute, before NADPH or O₂ was significantly depleted.\n\nFigure 1 shows the results. Every enzyme turnover consumes one NADPH and one O₂, whether it ends in Reaction 3a or Reaction 3b. In the absence of any ligand, H₂O₂ formed at only 1 μM·min⁻¹. No hydroxylated product of 6-HN was detected at any concentration.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Rates of hydroxylated product formation and H₂O₂ formation by PHBH as a function of ligand concentration","x":{"label":"[Ligand] (μM)","min":0,"max":400,"step":100},"y":{"label":"Rate (μM·min⁻¹)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"pOHB → product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[25,14],[50,22],[100,30],[200,36],[300,38],[400,39]]},{"name":"pOHB → H₂O₂","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1],[25,1],[50,1],[100,1],[200,2],[300,2],[400,2]]},{"name":"pAB → product","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[25,6],[50,10],[100,15],[200,18],[300,20],[400,20]]},{"name":"pAB → H₂O₂","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1],[25,4],[50,7],[100,10],[200,12],[300,13],[400,14]]},{"name":"6-HN → H₂O₂","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1],[25,8],[50,13],[100,20],[200,26],[300,28],[400,29]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"At 100 μM ligand, at what rate does PHBH hydroxylate pOHB relative to the rate at which it uncouples to form H₂O₂ in the presence of 6-HN?","choices":["0.67","1.5","2.0","3.0"],"ans":1,"exp":"Hydroxylation of pOHB at 100 μM is 30 μM·min⁻¹ and H₂O₂ formation with 6-HN is 20 μM·min⁻¹, so the ratio is 30/20 = **1.5**. **Pick the curve whose y-axis quantity matches each half of the question.**","how":{"ask":"Find the two curves that describe 'hydroxylating pOHB' and 'uncoupling with 6-HN', read both at 100 μM, and divide in the order asked.","know":"• Hydroxylation = Reaction 3a, measured as product formation.\n• Uncoupling = Reaction 3b, measured as H₂O₂ formation.\n• 'A relative to B' means A ÷ B.","steps":[{"text":"Hydroxylating pOHB = product formation; uncoupling = H₂O₂ formation.","hl":"FADH–OOH instead decomposes to FAD and H₂O₂ (Reaction 3b), a process called uncoupling"},{"text":"Read ● pOHB → product at 100 μM: 30 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["pOHB → product"],"read":[{"series":"pOHB → product","x":100}],"xLine":100}},{"text":"Read ◆ 6-HN → H₂O₂ at 100 μM: 20 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["pOHB → product","6-HN → H₂O₂"],"read":[{"series":"6-HN → H₂O₂","x":100}],"xLine":100}},"Divide pOHB hydroxylation by 6-HN uncoupling: 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5."],"trap":"2.0 comes from reading ■ pAB → product (15) instead of ◆ 6-HN → H₂O₂ (20) as the denominator; 0.67 divides the wrong way."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"With 200 μM pAB present, what is the total rate at which PHBH consumes O₂?","choices":["12 μM·min⁻¹","18 μM·min⁻¹","30 μM·min⁻¹","36 μM·min⁻¹"],"ans":2,"exp":"Each turnover uses one O₂ whether it ends in hydroxylation or H₂O₂ release, so O₂ use = 18 + 12 = **30 μM·min⁻¹**. **Total O₂ consumption = coupled + uncoupled turnovers.**","how":{"ask":"The figure never plots O₂ directly. Which plotted rates together account for every O₂ the enzyme uses?","know":"• Reaction 2 consumes one O₂ per turnover.\n• Each turnover then ends in either Reaction 3a (product) or 3b (H₂O₂), never both.\n• So O₂ rate = product rate + H₂O₂ rate.","steps":[{"text":"Every turnover uses one O₂, coupled or not.","hl":"Every enzyme turnover consumes one NADPH and one O₂, whether it ends in Reaction 3a or Reaction 3b"},{"text":"Read ■ pAB → product at 200 μM: 18 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["pAB → product","pAB → H₂O₂"],"read":[{"series":"pAB → product","x":200}],"xLine":200}},{"text":"Read □ pAB → H₂O₂ at 200 μM: 12 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["pAB → product","pAB → H₂O₂"],"read":[{"series":"pAB → H₂O₂","x":200}],"xLine":200}},"Add: 18 + 12 = 30 μM·min⁻¹ of O₂."],"trap":"18 μM·min⁻¹ counts only the coupled turnovers; the uncoupled turnovers that release H₂O₂ consume O₂ too. 36 is the pOHB curve at 200 μM."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"After one complete uncoupled turnover in the presence of 6-HN, which substance has undergone net reduction?","choices":["O₂","FAD","NADP⁺","6-HN"],"ans":0,"exp":"O₂ (oxidation state 0) becomes H₂O₂ (oxygen −1), so **O₂ is the net acceptor of NADPH's electrons**; FAD is reduced then reoxidized, so it is regenerated unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Follow electrons from NADPH through Reactions 1, 2 and 3b and ask where they end up after the cycle is over.","know":"• Reduction = gain of electrons (or hydride/H).\n• A species that is reduced and then reoxidized in the same cycle shows no net change (a regenerated cofactor).\n• Oxygen oxidation state: O₂ = 0, H₂O₂ = −1.","steps":[{"text":"Reaction 1: NADPH gives a hydride to FAD; NADPH is oxidized to NADP⁺.","hl":"NADPH transfers a hydride to the enzyme-bound FAD, forming FADH⁻ and NADP⁺ (Reaction 1)"},{"text":"Reaction 2: FADH⁻ passes the electrons to O₂.","hl":"FADH⁻ then reacts with O₂ to form a C4a-hydroperoxyflavin, FADH–OOH (Reaction 2)"},{"text":"Reaction 3b: FAD is regenerated and O₂ ends as H₂O₂, so O₂ is net reduced.","hl":"FADH–OOH instead decomposes to FAD and H₂O₂ (Reaction 3b)"},{"text":"6-HN is not changed; its only role is to trigger Reaction 1.","hl":"No hydroxylated product of 6-HN was detected at any concentration","graph":{"focus":["6-HN → H₂O₂"]}}],"trap":"FAD is reduced in Reaction 1 but reoxidized in Reaction 3b, so it shows no net change. NADP⁺ is the oxidized product of NADPH, not a reduced species."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"Coupling efficiency is the fraction of turnovers that produce hydroxylated product. What is the coupling efficiency with 400 μM pAB?","choices":["70%","41%","143%","59%"],"ans":3,"exp":"Coupling = product ÷ (product + H₂O₂) = 20 ÷ (20 + 14) = **59%**. **The denominator is all turnovers, coupled plus uncoupled.**","how":{"ask":"Convert two curve readings into the fraction of all turnovers that end in hydroxylation.","know":"• All turnovers = coupled (product) + uncoupled (H₂O₂).\n• Fraction coupled = coupled ÷ all turnovers.","steps":[{"text":"Read ■ pAB → product at 400 μM: 20 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["pAB → product","pAB → H₂O₂"],"read":[{"series":"pAB → product","x":400}],"xLine":400}},{"text":"Read □ pAB → H₂O₂ at 400 μM: 14 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["pAB → product","pAB → H₂O₂"],"read":[{"series":"pAB → H₂O₂","x":400}],"xLine":400}},{"text":"Total turnovers = 20 + 14 = 34; coupled fraction = 20/34 ≈ 0.59.","hl":"Coupling efficiency is the fraction of turnovers that produce hydroxylated product"}],"trap":"70% is H₂O₂ ÷ product (14/20), which compares the two pathways to each other instead of dividing by total turnovers; 143% is the inverse."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which conclusion about 6-HN is best supported by the passage and Figure 1?","choices":["6-HN competes with NADPH and slows flavin reduction","6-HN prevents O₂ from reacting with the reduced flavin","6-HN binding speeds flavin reduction but 6-HN is not hydroxylated","6-HN is hydroxylated faster than pOHB at high concentrations"],"ans":2,"exp":"H₂O₂ formation rises from 1 to 29 μM·min⁻¹ as 6-HN increases, so 6-HN **turns on Reactions 1 and 2**, yet no product forms. **An effector that is not a substrate causes uncoupling.**","how":{"ask":"Compare the 6-HN H₂O₂ curve with the no-ligand rate, then combine that with what the passage says about 6-HN products.","know":"• H₂O₂ is made only after Reactions 1 and 2 occur.\n• If H₂O₂ formation increases with a ligand, the ligand must speed the cycle up to FADH–OOH.\n• No product means the ligand is not hydroxylated.","steps":[{"text":"Without ligand, H₂O₂ forms at only 1 μM·min⁻¹.","hl":"In the absence of any ligand, H₂O₂ formed at only 1 μM·min⁻¹"},{"text":"Read ◆ 6-HN → H₂O₂ at 0 and 400 μM: 1 → 29 μM·min⁻¹; turnover is strongly stimulated.","graph":{"focus":["6-HN → H₂O₂"],"read":[{"series":"6-HN → H₂O₂","x":0},{"series":"6-HN → H₂O₂","x":400}]}},{"text":"Yet no 6-HN product forms, so all of these turnovers are uncoupled.","hl":"No hydroxylated product of 6-HN was detected at any concentration"}],"trap":"Slowing flavin reduction or blocking O₂ would lower H₂O₂ production below 1 μM·min⁻¹, but it rises nearly 30-fold. No 6-HN product was detected, so it is not hydroxylated at all."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"PHBH hydroxylates pOHB at C3, the ring carbon ortho to the hydroxyl group, through electrophilic aromatic substitution. Which statement best explains this regiochemistry?","choices":["The carboxylate group donates electron density to the ortho carbons","The distal oxygen of FADH–OOH acts as a nucleophile toward the ring","The carbon ortho to the hydroxyl carries the largest partial positive charge","The phenolate oxygen donates electron density to the ortho carbons"],"ans":3,"exp":"An –O⁻/–OH group is a strong **ortho/para-directing activator** because its lone pairs delocalize into the ring; the para position is occupied by the carboxylate, so attack occurs ortho, at C3.","how":{"ask":"Use organic chemistry: which ring substituent controls where an electrophile attacks?","know":"• In electrophilic aromatic substitution, an electron-poor oxygen attacks an electron-rich ring carbon.\n• Groups with lone pairs on the atom bonded to the ring, such as –OH or –O⁻, donate by resonance and direct ortho/para.\n• –COOH/–COO⁻ withdraws by resonance and directs meta.","steps":[{"text":"The reaction is electrophilic aromatic substitution: the ring is the nucleophile.","hl":"through electrophilic aromatic substitution"},{"text":"The oxygen that reaches the ring is the electrophilic distal oxygen of FADH–OOH.","hl":"the distal oxygen of FADH–OOH is transferred to the ring"},"Phenolate lone pairs push electron density to the ortho and para carbons; para is blocked by the carboxylate, so C3 is attacked."],"trap":"The carboxylate choice names the wrong group: carboxyl groups withdraw electrons and direct meta. A carbon with a large partial positive charge would repel, not attract, an electrophile."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_biochem_2","subj":"biochemistry","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biochemistry","title":"Inhibition of Alcohol Dehydrogenase by Fomepizole and Compound Y","passage":"Alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) catalyzes the first step of ethanol metabolism in the liver: ethanol + NAD⁺ → acetaldehyde + NADH + H⁺. The same enzyme oxidizes methanol to formaldehyde, which is further oxidized to formic acid, the metabolite responsible for the metabolic acidosis and optic nerve damage of methanol poisoning.\n\nResearchers purified human liver ADH and measured initial velocity, v, by following the increase in absorbance at 340 nm, a wavelength at which NADH absorbs but NAD⁺ does not. Each reaction contained 2 mM NAD⁺, a fixed amount of enzyme, and 0–40 mM ethanol. Reactions were run with no inhibitor, with 4-methylpyrazole (fomepizole), a drug used to treat methanol poisoning, or with Compound Y, a synthetic molecule identified in a drug screen. Each inhibitor was present at a single fixed concentration. All assays were performed at pH 7.5 and 25 °C, and each point is the mean of three trials.\n\nFigure 1 shows the results. Values of v are expressed as micromoles of NADH formed per liter per minute. The researchers defined Vmax as the plateau value that v approaches at saturating ethanol and KM as the ethanol concentration at which v equals half of Vmax for the same curve. Inhibitors that bind only the free enzyme raise the apparent KM, whereas inhibitors that bind the free enzyme and the enzyme–substrate complex equally lower Vmax.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Initial velocity of human liver ADH as a function of ethanol concentration with and without inhibitors","x":{"label":"[Ethanol] (mM)","min":0,"max":40,"step":5},"y":{"label":"v (μM NADH·min⁻¹)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,25],[2,33.3],[4,40],[8,44.4],[12,46.2],[20,47.6],[30,48.4],[40,48.8]]},{"name":"Fomepizole","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,10],[2,16.7],[4,25],[8,33.3],[12,37.5],[20,41.7],[30,44.1],[40,45.5]]},{"name":"Compound Y","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,12.5],[2,16.7],[4,20],[8,22.2],[12,23.1],[20,23.8],[30,24.2],[40,24.4]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"What is the KM of ADH for ethanol in the absence of inhibitor?","choices":["0.5 mM","1 mM","4 mM","25 mM"],"ans":1,"exp":"The uninhibited curve plateaus near 50 μM·min⁻¹, so half-Vmax is 25 μM·min⁻¹, reached at **1 mM ethanol**. **KM is an x-value: the [S] giving v = Vmax/2.**","how":{"ask":"First find the plateau of the uninhibited curve, halve it, then find the x-value where the curve reaches that height.","know":"• Vmax = maximal rate when enzyme is saturated with substrate.\n• KM = substrate concentration at which v = ½Vmax; it is read on the x-axis.","steps":[{"text":"KM is the ethanol concentration where v = ½Vmax.","hl":"KM as the ethanol concentration at which v equals half of Vmax for the same curve"},{"text":"● No inhibitor levels off near 50 μM·min⁻¹ (48.8 at 40 mM), so Vmax ≈ 50 and ½Vmax = 25.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor"],"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":40}],"yLine":50}},{"text":"● reaches 25 μM·min⁻¹ at 1 mM ethanol.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor"],"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":1}],"yLine":25,"xLine":1}}],"trap":"25 mM confuses the y-value at half-Vmax with the x-value; 4 mM is the KM read from the fomepizole curve."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"By what factor does fomepizole change the apparent KM of ADH for ethanol?","choices":["0.25","1.6","4","8"],"ans":2,"exp":"With fomepizole, Vmax is still ≈ 50, and v = 25 μM·min⁻¹ at 4 mM, so apparent KM = 4 mM vs 1 mM without inhibitor: a **4-fold increase**.","how":{"ask":"Find KM on each of two curves using each curve's own Vmax, then compare them as a ratio.","know":"• Apparent KM = [S] giving half of that curve's Vmax.\n• Factor = KM with inhibitor ÷ KM without inhibitor.","steps":[{"text":"Fomepizole curve approaches the same plateau, ≈ 50 μM·min⁻¹ (45.5 at 40 mM and still rising).","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Fomepizole"],"read":[{"series":"Fomepizole","x":40}],"yLine":50}},{"text":"□ Fomepizole reaches 25 at 4 mM: apparent KM = 4 mM.","graph":{"focus":["Fomepizole"],"read":[{"series":"Fomepizole","x":4}],"yLine":25}},{"text":"● No inhibitor reaches 25 at 1 mM: KM = 1 mM.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Fomepizole"],"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":1}],"yLine":25}},"Factor = 4 mM ÷ 1 mM = 4."],"trap":"1.6 compares the two velocities at 4 mM (40 ÷ 25), not the two KM values; 0.25 divides the KMs the wrong way (1 ÷ 4)."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"At 4 mM ethanol, how many times faster is the uninhibited reaction than the reaction containing fomepizole?","choices":["2.0","0.63","15","1.6"],"ans":3,"exp":"At 4 mM, uninhibited v = 40 and fomepizole v = 25 μM·min⁻¹; 40 ÷ 25 = **1.6**. **'How many times faster' is a ratio, not a difference.**","how":{"ask":"Read the two named curves at the same x and divide the uninhibited value by the inhibited one.","know":"• Ratio = uninhibited ÷ inhibited when the question asks how many times faster the uninhibited reaction is.\n• A difference (subtraction) is not a 'times' comparison.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● No inhibitor at 4 mM: 40 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Fomepizole"],"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":4}],"xLine":4}},{"text":"Read □ Fomepizole at 4 mM: 25 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Fomepizole"],"read":[{"series":"Fomepizole","x":4}],"xLine":4}},"40 ÷ 25 = 1.6."],"trap":"2.0 reads ▲ Compound Y (20) instead of fomepizole; 15 is the difference 40 − 25; 0.63 divides the wrong way."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which classification of Compound Y is best supported by Figure 1?","choices":["Noncompetitive, because Vmax is halved while KM stays near 1 mM","Competitive, because v is lowered at every ethanol concentration","Uncompetitive, because Vmax and KM are both lowered by half","Noncompetitive, because KM rises fourfold while Vmax is unchanged"],"ans":0,"exp":"Compound Y's plateau is ≈ 25 (half of 50), and v = 12.5 = ½ of 25 at 1 mM, the same KM as uninhibited. **Lower Vmax with unchanged KM = noncompetitive (pure) inhibition.**","how":{"ask":"Get Vmax and KM for the Compound Y curve and compare each with the uninhibited values.","know":"• Competitive: KM↑, Vmax unchanged.\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax↓, KM unchanged (binds E and ES equally).\n• Uncompetitive: both Vmax and KM decrease.","steps":[{"text":"▲ Compound Y levels off near 25 μM·min⁻¹ (24.4 at 40 mM): Vmax halved.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Compound Y"],"read":[{"series":"Compound Y","x":40}],"yLine":25}},{"text":"▲ at 1 mM: 12.5 μM·min⁻¹ = ½ × 25, so KM = 1 mM, unchanged.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Compound Y"],"read":[{"series":"Compound Y","x":1}],"xLine":1,"yLine":12.5}},{"text":"Lower Vmax with the same KM fits an inhibitor binding E and ES equally.","hl":"inhibitors that bind the free enzyme and the enzyme–substrate complex equally lower Vmax"}],"trap":"'Competitive, because v is lowered' gives a true observation with the wrong conclusion: every inhibitor lowers v. The fourfold KM rise belongs to fomepizole, not Compound Y."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Without inhibitor at 8 mM ethanol, at what rate is ethanol oxidized, and which species is reduced in the reaction?","choices":["44 μM·min⁻¹, and ethanol is reduced","22 μM·min⁻¹, and NAD⁺ is reduced","44 μM·min⁻¹, and NAD⁺ is reduced","89 μM·min⁻¹, and NAD⁺ is reduced"],"ans":2,"exp":"v is measured as NADH formed, and the reaction makes one NADH per ethanol, so ethanol is oxidized at **44 μM·min⁻¹**; NAD⁺ accepts a hydride and is **reduced** to NADH.","how":{"ask":"The y-axis reports NADH formation, not ethanol use. Use the reaction stoichiometry to convert, then decide who gains electrons.","know":"• ethanol + NAD⁺ → acetaldehyde + NADH + H⁺: 1 : 1 stoichiometry.\n• Ethanol loses H (oxidized to acetaldehyde); NAD⁺ gains a hydride (reduced).","steps":[{"text":"The y-axis measures NADH formation.","hl":"Values of v are expressed as micromoles of NADH formed per liter per minute"},{"text":"Stoichiometry: one NADH per ethanol oxidized.","hl":"ethanol + NAD⁺ → acetaldehyde + NADH + H⁺"},{"text":"Read ● No inhibitor at 8 mM: 44.4 ≈ 44 μM·min⁻¹.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor"],"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":8}],"xLine":8}},"Ethanol is oxidized; NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH."],"trap":"'Ethanol is reduced' has the right rate but wrong redox: ethanol becomes an aldehyde, an oxidation. 22 is the Compound Y curve at 8 mM; 89 doubles the stoichiometry."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"A patient who ingested methanol is treated with fomepizole. Which outcome best explains the therapeutic benefit?","choices":["Less formic acid forms, limiting the anion gap acidosis","Less NADH forms, so lactate production rises","Methanol is oxidized faster and excreted as formaldehyde","ADH Vmax rises, so ethanol cannot compete with methanol"],"ans":0,"exp":"Fomepizole competitively blocks ADH, so methanol is cleared unchanged by the kidney instead of being converted to formate. **Fewer unmeasured anions → less anion gap metabolic acidosis.**","how":{"ask":"Link the inhibitor type seen in Figure 1 to the toxic pathway the passage describes.","know":"• Methanol → formaldehyde → formic acid; formate is an unmeasured anion that raises the anion gap.\n• A competitive inhibitor slows ADH unless the alcohol concentration is very high.","steps":[{"text":"The toxic product is formic acid, made downstream of ADH.","hl":"formic acid, the metabolite responsible for the metabolic acidosis"},{"text":"Figure 1: fomepizole lowers v at every alcohol concentration tested, most at low concentrations (4 mM: 25 vs 40).","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","Fomepizole"],"read":[{"series":"Fomepizole","x":4},{"series":"No inhibitor","x":4}]}},"Slower methanol oxidation → less formic acid → less acidosis."],"trap":"Fomepizole slows, not speeds, oxidation, so 'methanol is oxidized faster' contradicts Figure 1. Fomepizole does not raise Vmax; its plateau is unchanged."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_biochem_3","subj":"biochemistry","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biochemistry","title":"Oxygen Binding by Hemoglobin, a High-Affinity Variant, and Myoglobin","passage":"Hemoglobin (Hb) is a tetramer of two α and two β chains, each carrying a heme whose Fe²⁺ binds one O₂. Binding is cooperative: O₂ binding to one subunit favors the shift from the low-affinity T state to the high-affinity R state, giving a sigmoidal saturation curve. Myoglobin (Mb), a monomeric muscle protein, binds O₂ noncooperatively. The partial pressure of O₂ at which a protein is 50% saturated is its P50; a lower P50 means higher affinity. A shift of the Hb curve to the right, as produced by H⁺, CO₂, or 2,3-BPG, lowers affinity and favors O₂ release in tissues.\n\nResearchers measured the percent O₂ saturation of purified proteins at 37 °C across a range of pO₂ values. Normal adult hemoglobin (HbA) was tested at pH 7.4 and at pH 7.2, each with a physiological concentration of 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG). A variant hemoglobin carrying a single β-chain substitution at the α1β2 subunit interface was tested at pH 7.4, as was Mb.\n\nFigure 1 shows the results. In a resting person, arterial blood leaving the lungs has a pO₂ of about 100 mmHg, and venous blood leaving the tissues has a pO₂ of about 40 mmHg. The fraction of its O₂ that Hb releases as blood passes through tissue therefore equals the drop in saturation between these two pO₂ values. Each gram of fully saturated Hb carries 1.34 mL of O₂, and a typical blood Hb concentration is 15 g/dL.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  O₂ saturation curves of HbA at two pH values, a β-chain variant Hb, and Mb at 37 °C","x":{"label":"pO₂ (mmHg)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"O₂ saturation (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"HbA, pH 7.4","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[5,1],[10,7],[15,18],[20,33],[26,50],[32,64],[40,76],[50,85],[60,91],[80,95],[100,97]]},{"name":"HbA, pH 7.2","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[5,1],[10,4],[15,11],[20,22],[26,36],[32,50],[40,65],[50,77],[60,85],[80,92],[100,96]]},{"name":"Variant Hb, pH 7.4","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[5,17],[10,42],[12,50],[20,71],[26,80],[32,85],[40,90],[60,95],[80,97],[100,98]]},{"name":"Mb, pH 7.4","marker":"diamond-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,25],[3,50],[5,62],[10,77],[20,87],[40,93],[60,95],[80,96],[100,97]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"What is the P50 of HbA at pH 7.2?","choices":["26 mmHg","32 mmHg","40 mmHg","65 mmHg"],"ans":1,"exp":"The pH 7.2 curve crosses 50% saturation at **32 mmHg**. **P50 is read on the x-axis where y = 50%.**","how":{"ask":"Locate the correct curve and find the x-value where it reaches half saturation.","know":"• P50 = pO₂ (x-axis) at which saturation (y-axis) = 50%.\n• Lower P50 = higher affinity.","steps":[{"text":"P50 is the pO₂ at 50% saturation.","hl":"The partial pressure of O₂ at which a protein is 50% saturated is its P50"},{"text":"Draw a line at 50% and find where □ HbA, pH 7.2 meets it: 32 mmHg.","graph":{"focus":["HbA, pH 7.2"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.2","x":32}],"yLine":50}}],"trap":"26 mmHg is the P50 of the pH 7.4 curve. 65 is a saturation value, the pH 7.2 curve's y at 40 mmHg, not a pO₂."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Compared with HbA at pH 7.4, how much more or less of its bound O₂ does HbA at pH 7.2 release between 100 mmHg and 40 mmHg?","choices":["10 percentage points more","11 percentage points less","21 percentage points more","31 percentage points more"],"ans":0,"exp":"pH 7.4: 97 − 76 = 21 points; pH 7.2: 96 − 65 = 31 points; difference **10 points more** at the lower pH (Bohr effect). **Unloading = saturation at 100 minus saturation at 40.**","how":{"ask":"Compute unloading for each curve as a drop in y between two x-values, then compare the two drops.","know":"• Unloading = sat(lungs, 100 mmHg) − sat(tissue, 40 mmHg).\n• Bohr effect: lower pH lowers affinity, shifting the curve right and increasing unloading.","steps":[{"text":"Unloading = drop in saturation from 100 to 40 mmHg.","hl":"equals the drop in saturation between these two pO₂ values"},{"text":"● pH 7.4: 97% at 100, 76% at 40 → 21 points.","graph":{"focus":["HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":100},{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":40}]}},{"text":"□ pH 7.2: 96% at 100, 65% at 40 → 31 points.","graph":{"focus":["HbA, pH 7.4","HbA, pH 7.2"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.2","x":100},{"series":"HbA, pH 7.2","x":40}]}},"31 − 21 = 10 percentage points more."],"trap":"11 points less compares only the two curves at 40 mmHg (76 − 65) and ignores loading at 100 mmHg; 31 is the pH 7.2 unloading alone."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"For blood containing HbA at pH 7.4, about how much O₂ is delivered to tissue per deciliter of blood as it passes from 100 mmHg to 40 mmHg?","choices":["19.5 mL/dL","15.3 mL/dL","4.2 mL/dL","2.1 mL/dL"],"ans":2,"exp":"Capacity = 15 g/dL × 1.34 mL/g = 20.1 mL/dL; unloading fraction = 0.97 − 0.76 = 0.21; 0.21 × 20.1 ≈ **4.2 mL/dL**. **Saturation is a percentage; multiply by capacity to get O₂ content.**","how":{"ask":"The y-axis is percent saturation, not mL of O₂. Convert the saturation drop into a volume using the passage's numbers.","know":"• O₂ capacity = [Hb] × 1.34 mL O₂/g.\n• O₂ delivered = capacity × (sat₁₀₀ − sat₄₀).","steps":[{"text":"Capacity = 15 × 1.34 = 20.1 mL O₂ per dL.","hl":"Each gram of fully saturated Hb carries 1.34 mL of O₂, and a typical blood Hb concentration is 15 g/dL"},{"text":"● HbA, pH 7.4: 97% at 100 mmHg and 76% at 40 mmHg.","graph":{"focus":["HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":100},{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":40}]}},"Delivered = 20.1 × 0.21 ≈ 4.2 mL/dL."],"trap":"15.3 mL/dL is the O₂ still bound at 40 mmHg (0.76 × 20.1), the venous content, not the amount delivered; 19.5 is the arterial content."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"A person is heterozygous for the variant Hb shown in Figure 1. Which physiological response is most consistent with the data?","choices":["Tissue O₂ delivery rises because the variant loads more O₂","Erythropoietin secretion falls because arterial saturation is higher","Ventilation falls because the variant releases O₂ more readily","Erythropoietin secretion rises, increasing red cell mass"],"ans":3,"exp":"The variant releases only 98 − 90 = 8 points between 100 and 40 mmHg, vs 21 for HbA, so tissues, including the kidney, sense **relative hypoxia → more EPO → erythrocytosis**.","how":{"ask":"Use the variant curve to judge how well it unloads O₂, then predict how the body compensates.","know":"• High-affinity Hb (low P50) loads well but unloads poorly.\n• Renal O₂ sensing: low tissue O₂ → HIF → EPO → more red cells.","steps":[{"text":"▲ Variant: P50 = 12 mmHg, far left of HbA (26 mmHg).","graph":{"focus":["Variant Hb, pH 7.4","HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"Variant Hb, pH 7.4","x":12}],"yLine":50}},{"text":"▲ Variant: 98% at 100 mmHg, 90% at 40 mmHg → only 8 points unloaded.","graph":{"focus":["Variant Hb, pH 7.4","HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"Variant Hb, pH 7.4","x":100},{"series":"Variant Hb, pH 7.4","x":40}]}},{"text":"● HbA: 97 → 76%, 21 points unloaded; variant delivers far less O₂.","graph":{"focus":["Variant Hb, pH 7.4","HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":40}]}},"Poor tissue delivery → kidney releases more EPO → more red cells."],"trap":"'Loads more O₂' is nearly irrelevant (98% vs 97% at 100 mmHg); what matters is unloading, which drops from 21 to 8 points. Higher arterial saturation does not lower EPO because the kidney senses tissue O₂."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"Fetal hemoglobin, with two α and two γ chains, binds 2,3-BPG more weakly than HbA does. Relative to the HbA, pH 7.4 curve, the fetal Hb curve under the same conditions:","choices":["is shifted right, with a P50 above 26 mmHg","is shifted left, with a P50 below 26 mmHg","is hyperbolic, with the same P50 as the Mb curve","is unchanged, because 2,3-BPG does not alter affinity"],"ans":1,"exp":"2,3-BPG binds and stabilizes the T state, lowering affinity. **Weaker BPG binding → higher affinity → left shift, P50 below 26 mmHg**, letting fetal blood take O₂ from maternal blood.","how":{"ask":"Recall what 2,3-BPG does to Hb affinity, then reason about a protein that binds it less.","know":"• 2,3-BPG binds the central cavity of deoxy (T-state) Hb and stabilizes it.\n• Less BPG effect = more R-like = higher affinity = lower P50 = curve shifted left.","steps":[{"text":"Fetal Hb binds 2,3-BPG weakly, so its T state is less stabilized.","hl":"binds 2,3-BPG more weakly than HbA does"},{"text":"HbA, pH 7.4 reference: P50 = 26 mmHg.","graph":{"focus":["HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":26}],"yLine":50}},"Higher affinity → curve moves left → P50 below 26 mmHg; fetal Hb is still a cooperative tetramer, so it stays sigmoidal."],"trap":"A right shift would follow from MORE BPG binding. Fetal Hb is a tetramer and remains cooperative, so it does not become hyperbolic like Mb."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"At a pO₂ of 20 mmHg, as in actively contracting muscle, what is the ratio of Mb saturation to HbA saturation at pH 7.4?","choices":["54","0.38","2.6","3.9"],"ans":2,"exp":"Mb is 87% saturated and HbA (pH 7.4) is 33% saturated at 20 mmHg: 87 ÷ 33 ≈ **2.6**. This large difference lets Mb pull O₂ from Hb in muscle.","how":{"ask":"Read two named curves at one x and divide Mb by HbA.","know":"• Ratio asked = Mb ÷ HbA.\n• Mb has P50 ≈ 3 mmHg (hyperbolic) and remains mostly saturated at low pO₂.","steps":[{"text":"Read ◇ Mb at 20 mmHg: 87%.","graph":{"focus":["Mb, pH 7.4","HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"Mb, pH 7.4","x":20}],"xLine":20}},{"text":"Read ● HbA, pH 7.4 at 20 mmHg: 33%.","graph":{"focus":["Mb, pH 7.4","HbA, pH 7.4"],"read":[{"series":"HbA, pH 7.4","x":20}],"xLine":20}},"87 ÷ 33 ≈ 2.6."],"trap":"3.9 divides by the pH 7.2 curve (87 ÷ 22) instead of pH 7.4; 0.38 inverts the ratio; 54 is the difference."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_biochem_4","subj":"biochemistry","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biochemistry","title":"Hormonal Control of Fructose 2,6-Bisphosphate in Hepatocytes","passage":"In hepatocytes, the bifunctional enzyme PFK-2/FBPase-2 controls the level of fructose 2,6-bisphosphate (F2,6BP). Its kinase domain catalyzes fructose 6-phosphate + ATP → F2,6BP + ADP (Reaction 1), and its phosphatase domain catalyzes F2,6BP + H₂O → fructose 6-phosphate + Pᵢ (Reaction 2). F2,6BP is a potent allosteric regulator of the committed steps of glycolysis and gluconeogenesis. Glucagon raises intracellular cAMP, which activates protein kinase A (PKA). When PKA phosphorylates Ser32 of the liver isoform, kinase activity falls and phosphatase activity rises. Insulin opposes this effect, in part by activating protein phosphatases that remove the phosphate from Ser32.\n\nTo test whether Ser32 phosphorylation is required for hormonal control of F2,6BP, researchers isolated hepatocytes from three mouse lines: wild type (WT), a knock-in line in which Ser32 is replaced by alanine (S32A), and a liver-specific knockout lacking the enzyme entirely (KO). Cells were incubated for 20 minutes in 10 mM glucose alone (Basal), with glucagon, with glucagon plus H-89, a PKA inhibitor, or with insulin.\n\nF2,6BP was then extracted and measured with an enzyme-coupled assay; KO cells served as a control for the specificity of the assay. Figure 1 shows mean values ± SD from four independent preparations. Immunoblots showed similar amounts of PFK-2/FBPase-2 protein in WT and S32A cells under every condition. The researchers considered two means different only if their error bars did not overlap.","figure":{"type":"bar","caption":"Figure 1  F2,6BP content of WT, S32A and KO hepatocytes after hormone treatments (mean ± SD, n = 4)","x":{"label":"Treatment"},"y":{"label":"F2,6BP (nmol/g cells)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"categories":["Basal","Glucagon","Glucagon + H-89","Insulin"],"series":[{"name":"WT","values":[10,2,9,14],"errors":[1,0.5,1,1.5]},{"name":"S32A","values":[12,11,12,13],"errors":[1,1.2,1,1.2]},{"name":"KO","values":[0.5,0.4,0.5,0.6],"errors":[0.2,0.2,0.2,0.2]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"By what percentage does glucagon lower F2,6BP in WT hepatocytes relative to Basal?","choices":["20%","80%","83%","500%"],"ans":1,"exp":"WT falls from 10 to 2 nmol/g: (10 − 2) ÷ 10 = **80%**. **Percent decrease = change ÷ starting value.**","how":{"ask":"Compare two WT bars and express the drop as a fraction of the Basal value.","know":"• % decrease = (initial − final) ÷ initial × 100.\n• Basal is the starting reference.","steps":[{"text":"WT Basal bar: 10 nmol/g.","graph":{"focus":["WT"],"bars":[["Basal","WT"]],"yLine":10}},{"text":"WT Glucagon bar: 2 nmol/g.","graph":{"focus":["WT"],"bars":[["Basal","WT"],["Glucagon","WT"]],"yLine":2}},"(10 − 2) ÷ 10 = 0.80 = 80%."],"trap":"20% is the remaining fraction (2 ÷ 10), not the decrease; 83% uses the S32A Basal bar (12) as the start value."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"In glucagon-treated cells, what is the ratio of F2,6BP in S32A hepatocytes to that in WT hepatocytes?","choices":["0.18","1.1","5.5","9.0"],"ans":2,"exp":"S32A with glucagon = 11, WT with glucagon = 2: 11 ÷ 2 = **5.5**. Blocking Ser32 phosphorylation prevents the glucagon-induced drop.","how":{"ask":"Read two bars in the same treatment group and divide S32A by WT.","know":"• Ratio of A to B = A ÷ B.\n• Compare bars within one category on the x-axis.","steps":[{"text":"S32A Glucagon bar: 11 nmol/g.","graph":{"focus":["S32A"],"bars":[["Glucagon","S32A"]],"yLine":11}},{"text":"WT Glucagon bar: 2 nmol/g.","graph":{"focus":["S32A","WT"],"bars":[["Glucagon","S32A"],["Glucagon","WT"]],"yLine":2}},"11 ÷ 2 = 5.5."],"trap":"9.0 is the difference 11 − 2; 1.1 divides S32A Glucagon by WT Basal, the wrong bar; 0.18 inverts the ratio."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"Based on the WT data, glucagon treatment most likely causes which change in hepatocyte enzyme activity?","choices":["PFK-1 activity rises and FBPase-1 activity falls","PFK-1 and FBPase-1 activities both rise","Hexokinase activity rises and FBPase-1 activity falls","PFK-1 activity falls and FBPase-1 activity rises"],"ans":3,"exp":"F2,6BP **activates PFK-1 and inhibits FBPase-1**. Glucagon cuts WT F2,6BP from 10 to 2, so glycolysis slows and gluconeogenesis speeds up, which fits glucagon's role in fasting.","how":{"ask":"Use outside knowledge of what F2,6BP does to the two opposing enzymes, then apply the direction of change in WT.","know":"• PFK-1: committed step of glycolysis (F6P → F1,6BP), activated by F2,6BP.\n• FBPase-1: gluconeogenic reverse step, inhibited by F2,6BP.\n• Low F2,6BP favors gluconeogenesis.","steps":[{"text":"F2,6BP regulates the opposing committed steps.","hl":"F2,6BP is a potent allosteric regulator of the committed steps of glycolysis and gluconeogenesis"},{"text":"WT: Basal 10 → Glucagon 2 nmol/g, so F2,6BP falls sharply.","graph":{"focus":["WT"],"bars":[["Basal","WT"],["Glucagon","WT"]]}},"Less F2,6BP → less PFK-1 activation and less FBPase-1 inhibition."],"trap":"'PFK-1 rises and FBPase-1 falls' is what happens when F2,6BP rises, as with insulin, not glucagon."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"What do the Glucagon + H-89 results in WT hepatocytes indicate?","choices":["Glucagon lowers F2,6BP in WT cells through PKA activity","H-89 raises F2,6BP by directly activating the kinase domain","Glucagon lowers F2,6BP through a pathway independent of PKA","Ser32 is phosphorylated even when PKA is inhibited"],"ans":0,"exp":"With H-89, WT F2,6BP stays at 9 ± 1, overlapping Basal 10 ± 1, so **blocking PKA abolishes the glucagon effect**: the effect requires PKA.","how":{"ask":"Compare the WT bar with glucagon alone to the WT bar with glucagon plus the PKA inhibitor, using Basal as the reference.","know":"• An inhibitor control tests whether a pathway is required: if blocking it removes the effect, the effect depends on it.\n• Glucagon → cAMP → PKA.","steps":[{"text":"H-89 is a PKA inhibitor.","hl":"with glucagon plus H-89, a PKA inhibitor"},{"text":"WT: Glucagon 2 vs Glucagon + H-89 9 vs Basal 10 nmol/g.","graph":{"focus":["WT"],"bars":[["Basal","WT"],["Glucagon","WT"],["Glucagon + H-89","WT"]]}},"Blocking PKA returns F2,6BP to Basal, so glucagon acts through PKA."],"trap":"'H-89 activates the kinase domain' cannot be concluded: there is no H-89-alone group, and the value (9) does not exceed Basal (10)."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"One molecule of fructose 6-phosphate is converted by Reaction 1 and then returned by Reaction 2. Which substances are consumed overall?","choices":["Fructose 6-phosphate and ATP","ATP and H₂O","F2,6BP and ADP","ADP and Pᵢ"],"ans":1,"exp":"Summing the reactions, F6P and F2,6BP cancel: **net ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ**, a futile cycle. F6P is regenerated, not consumed.","how":{"ask":"Add Reactions 1 and 2, cancel species that appear on both sides, and list what remains on the left.","know":"• In a cycle, an intermediate made in one step and used in the next cancels.\n• Net consumed = reactants left after cancellation.","steps":[{"text":"Reaction 1 uses F6P and ATP, making F2,6BP and ADP.","hl":"fructose 6-phosphate + ATP → F2,6BP + ADP (Reaction 1)"},{"text":"Reaction 2 uses F2,6BP and H₂O, regenerating F6P and making Pᵢ.","hl":"F2,6BP + H₂O → fructose 6-phosphate + Pᵢ (Reaction 2)"},"Cancel F6P and F2,6BP: net ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ."],"trap":"Fructose 6-phosphate is consumed in Reaction 1 but regenerated in Reaction 2, so it is not net consumed. ADP and Pᵢ are products."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"By the researchers' criterion, which pair of means is different?","choices":["S32A Glucagon and S32A Basal","S32A Insulin and S32A Basal","WT Insulin and WT Basal","WT Glucagon + H-89 and WT Basal"],"ans":2,"exp":"WT Insulin spans 12.5–15.5 and WT Basal spans 9–11: **no overlap, so different**. All the other pairs have overlapping error bars.","how":{"ask":"Convert each mean ± SD into a range and check which pair of ranges does not overlap.","know":"• Error bar range = mean − SD to mean + SD.\n• Criterion from the passage: different only if the bars do not overlap.","steps":[{"text":"Rule: different only if error bars do not overlap.","hl":"The researchers considered two means different only if their error bars did not overlap"},{"text":"WT Basal 10 ± 1 → 9–11; WT Insulin 14 ± 1.5 → 12.5–15.5: no overlap.","graph":{"focus":["WT"],"bars":[["Basal","WT"],["Insulin","WT"]]}},{"text":"S32A Basal 12 ± 1 → 11–13 overlaps Glucagon 11 ± 1.2 → 9.8–12.2 and Insulin 13 ± 1.2 → 11.8–14.2.","graph":{"focus":["S32A"],"bars":[["Basal","S32A"],["Glucagon","S32A"],["Insulin","S32A"]]}},{"text":"WT Glucagon + H-89 9 ± 1 → 8–10 overlaps WT Basal 9–11.","graph":{"focus":["WT"],"bars":[["Glucagon + H-89","WT"],["Basal","WT"]]}}],"trap":"S32A Insulin (13) looks higher than S32A Basal (12), but 11.8–14.2 overlaps 11–13, so by the stated criterion they are not different."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_bio_1","subj":"biology","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biology","title":"Two Ways to Block a Muscarinic Response","passage":"Acetylcholine (ACh) released from parasympathetic nerve endings contracts intestinal smooth muscle by binding M3 muscarinic receptors, which are coupled to the G protein Gq. A drug that reduces the response to an agonist is an antagonist. A competitive antagonist binds reversibly to the same site as the agonist, so a high enough agonist concentration can outcompete it. A noncompetitive antagonist acts at a different site or at a later step, so adding more agonist cannot overcome it. The potency of an agonist is reflected in its EC₅₀, whereas its efficacy is reflected in the maximal response it can produce.\n\nResearchers suspended strips of guinea pig ileum in an oxygenated salt bath and recorded isometric tension. ACh was added in cumulative steps, and each response was expressed as a percentage of the maximal tension produced by ACh in the same strip before any drug was added. After washout, strips were incubated for 30 min with atropine (10 nM or 100 nM) or with Compound N (1 μM), an experimental molecule that binds an allosteric site on the M3 receptor, and the ACh curve was repeated in the drug's presence. The EC₅₀ of a curve is the ACh concentration that gives half of that curve's own maximal response.\n\nFigure 1 shows the results. In a final experiment, strips exposed to both 10 nM atropine and 1 μM Compound N were to be tested, and the investigators predicted the shape of this curve before running it.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Ileum contraction versus log[ACh] alone and in the presence of atropine or Compound N","x":{"label":"log [ACh] (M)","min":-9,"max":-3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Tension (% of control maximum)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"ACh alone","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-9,1],[-8.5,3],[-8,9],[-7.5,24],[-7,50],[-6.5,76],[-6,91],[-5.5,97],[-5,99],[-4.5,100],[-4,100],[-3.5,100],[-3,100]]},{"name":"ACh + atropine 10 nM","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[-9,0],[-8.5,0],[-8,1],[-7.5,3],[-7,9],[-6.5,24],[-6,50],[-5.5,76],[-5,91],[-4.5,97],[-4,99],[-3.5,100],[-3,100]]},{"name":"ACh + atropine 100 nM","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[-9,0],[-8.5,0],[-8,0],[-7.5,0],[-7,1],[-6.5,3],[-6,9],[-5.5,24],[-5,50],[-4.5,76],[-4,91],[-3.5,97],[-3,99]]},{"name":"ACh + Compound N 1 μM","marker":"diamond-open","line":"smooth","points":[[-9,0],[-8.5,1],[-8,4],[-7.5,10],[-7,20],[-6.5,30],[-6,36],[-5.5,39],[-5,40],[-4.5,40],[-4,40],[-3.5,40],[-3,40]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"What is the EC₅₀ of ACh in the absence of any antagonist?","choices":["10 nM","100 nM","1 μM","10 μM"],"ans":1,"exp":"The ACh-alone curve reaches 50% of its own 100% maximum at log[ACh] = −7, so **EC₅₀ = 10⁻⁷ M = 100 nM**.","how":{"ask":"Find the x value where the no-drug curve is halfway to its own top, then convert the log value to a concentration.","know":"• EC₅₀ = agonist concentration giving half of that curve's maximal response.\n• The x-axis is log[ACh]; a value of −7 means [ACh] = 10⁻⁷ M.\n• 1 nM = 10⁻⁹ M, 1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M.","steps":[{"text":"Use the passage's definition of EC₅₀.","hl":"The EC₅₀ of a curve is the ACh concentration that gives half of that curve's own maximal response."},{"text":"The ● ACh-alone curve tops out at 100%, so half-maximum is 50%.","graph":{"focus":["ACh alone"],"yLine":50}},{"text":"Read ● at log[ACh] = −7: 50% of control maximum.","graph":{"focus":["ACh alone"],"read":[{"series":"ACh alone","x":-7}],"yLine":50}},"10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM = 100 nM."],"trap":"1 μM is where the ■ 10 nM atropine curve crosses 50%; that is the wrong curve. 10 nM comes from miscounting log units: 10⁻⁸ M."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"By what factor does 100 nM atropine increase the ACh concentration required for a half-maximal response?","choices":["2","10","100","1000"],"ans":2,"exp":"EC₅₀ moves from 10⁻⁷ M to 10⁻⁵ M; **fold shift = EC₅₀ with antagonist ÷ EC₅₀ without = 10⁻⁵/10⁻⁷ = 100**.","how":{"ask":"Compare the half-maximal ACh concentrations of two curves as a ratio, not as a difference on the log axis.","know":"• On a log axis, a shift of 1 unit is a 10-fold change in concentration.\n• Fold shift (dose ratio) = EC₅₀ in the presence of antagonist divided by EC₅₀ without it.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● ACh alone at 50%: log[ACh] = −7.","graph":{"focus":["ACh alone","ACh + atropine 100 nM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh alone","x":-7}],"yLine":50}},{"text":"Read ▲ 100 nM atropine at 50%: log[ACh] = −5.","graph":{"focus":["ACh alone","ACh + atropine 100 nM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh + atropine 100 nM","x":-5}],"yLine":50}},"The shift is −5 − (−7) = 2 log units.","Ratio = 10⁻⁵ M ÷ 10⁻⁷ M = 10² = 100."],"trap":"2 is the distance between the curves in log units, not the fold change; each log unit is a factor of 10."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"At 10 μM ACh, the tension produced in the presence of Compound N is what fraction of the tension produced by ACh alone at the same concentration?","choices":["2.5","0.60","0.40","0.20"],"ans":2,"exp":"At log[ACh] = −5, ◇ = 40% and ● = 99% of the same control maximum, so **Compound N ÷ ACh alone = 40/99 ≈ 0.40**.","how":{"ask":"Both curves share one y-scale; read each at the same x and divide the Compound N value by the ACh-alone value.","know":"• 10 μM = 10⁻⁵ M, so log[ACh] = −5.\n• Because every response is a percent of the same pre-drug maximum, the y-values can be compared directly.","steps":[{"text":"The y-axis is a percentage of the drug-free maximum in the same strip.","hl":"each response was expressed as a percentage of the maximal tension produced by ACh in the same strip before any drug was added"},{"text":"Read ◇ Compound N at log[ACh] = −5: 40%.","graph":{"focus":["ACh + Compound N 1 μM","ACh alone"],"read":[{"series":"ACh + Compound N 1 μM","x":-5}],"xLine":-5}},{"text":"Read ● ACh alone at log[ACh] = −5: 99%.","graph":{"focus":["ACh + Compound N 1 μM","ACh alone"],"read":[{"series":"ACh alone","x":-5}],"xLine":-5}},"40 ÷ 99 ≈ 0.40."],"trap":"0.20 comes from reading ◇ at log[ACh] = −7, the wrong x; 2.5 divides the curves the wrong way round."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which conclusion about the two antagonists is best supported by Figure 1?","choices":["Atropine is noncompetitive because it raises the ACh EC₅₀","Compound N is competitive because the ACh EC₅₀ is unchanged","Compound N is surmountable because tension rises with ACh","Atropine is surmountable because high ACh restores full tension"],"ans":3,"exp":"With atropine the curves shift right but still reach ~100%, while Compound N caps tension at 40%: **a competitive antagonist is surmountable; a noncompetitive one lowers the maximum**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which drug's effect disappears at high agonist concentration and which drug's effect does not.","know":"• Competitive antagonist: same site, reversible → rightward shift, same maximum.\n• Noncompetitive antagonist: different site/step → maximum falls; EC₅₀ may be unchanged.\n• Surmountable = overcome by more agonist.","steps":[{"text":"Recall the passage's distinction.","hl":"so a high enough agonist concentration can outcompete it"},{"text":"Read ▲ 100 nM atropine at log[ACh] = −3: 99%, essentially the full maximum.","graph":{"focus":["ACh + atropine 100 nM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh + atropine 100 nM","x":-3}]}},{"text":"Read ◇ Compound N at log[ACh] = −3: still only 40%.","graph":{"focus":["ACh + Compound N 1 μM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh + Compound N 1 μM","x":-3}],"yLine":40}},"Atropine is overcome by ACh; Compound N is not."],"trap":"The Compound N choice that says 'competitive because EC₅₀ is unchanged' uses the wrong criterion: an unchanged EC₅₀ with a lowered maximum is the noncompetitive pattern."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"A patient receives a systemic dose of atropine large enough to block muscarinic receptors throughout the body. Which combination of effects is expected?","choices":["Faster heart rate and slower gut motility","Slower heart rate and faster gut motility","Faster heart rate and more salivation","Constricted pupils and slower gut motility"],"ans":0,"exp":"Atropine blocks parasympathetic muscarinic signaling everywhere, so **vagal slowing of the SA node is removed and ACh-driven gut contraction is reduced**.","how":{"ask":"Apply the result in the ileum to every organ that receives parasympathetic muscarinic input.","know":"• Parasympathetic ('rest and digest'): slows heart via M2 receptors, increases gut motility and salivation, constricts pupils.\n• Blocking muscarinic receptors produces the opposite of each of these.","steps":[{"text":"ACh from parasympathetic nerves drives ileum contraction.","hl":"Acetylcholine (ACh) released from parasympathetic nerve endings contracts intestinal smooth muscle"},{"text":"At log[ACh] = −7, 10 nM atropine cuts tension from 50% to 9%: gut contraction is suppressed.","graph":{"focus":["ACh alone","ACh + atropine 10 nM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh alone","x":-7},{"series":"ACh + atropine 10 nM","x":-7}]}},"Removing vagal input to the heart raises heart rate; saliva drops and pupils dilate."],"trap":"'Faster heart rate and more salivation' is half right; salivation is also parasympathetic, so atropine causes dry mouth."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which prediction about the curve for strips exposed to both 10 nM atropine and 1 μM Compound N is most consistent with Figure 1?","choices":["Maximum 100%; half of maximum at 1 μM ACh","Maximum 40%; half of maximum at 0.1 μM ACh","Maximum 40%; half of maximum at 1 μM ACh","Maximum 40%; half of maximum at 10 μM ACh"],"ans":2,"exp":"Atropine alone shifts EC₅₀ 10-fold to 1 μM and Compound N alone caps tension at 40% without moving EC₅₀; **independent effects combine: max 40%, EC₅₀ 1 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Take what each drug does to the curve by itself and apply both changes to the control curve.","know":"• Competitive antagonist → changes EC₅₀ only.\n• Noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist here → changes maximum only.\n• EC₅₀ = concentration giving half of that curve's own maximum.","steps":[{"text":"The combined condition is described at the end of the passage.","hl":"strips exposed to both 10 nM atropine and 1 μM Compound N were to be tested"},{"text":"Read ■ 10 nM atropine at log[ACh] = −6: 50%, so atropine moves EC₅₀ to 1 μM.","graph":{"focus":["ACh + atropine 10 nM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh + atropine 10 nM","x":-6}],"yLine":50}},{"text":"Read ◇ Compound N at log[ACh] = −7: 20%, half of its 40% maximum, so EC₅₀ is unchanged; read ◇ at −3: 40% maximum.","graph":{"focus":["ACh + Compound N 1 μM"],"read":[{"series":"ACh + Compound N 1 μM","x":-7},{"series":"ACh + Compound N 1 μM","x":-3}]}},"Combine: maximum 40%, half-maximum of 20% at 10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM."],"trap":"'Maximum 100%; half of maximum at 1 μM' keeps only the atropine effect and forgets that more ACh cannot overcome Compound N."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_bio_2","subj":"biology","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biology","title":"Glucose and Insulin After an Oral Load","passage":"After a meal, rising plasma glucose enters pancreatic β cells through GLUT2 transporters. Glucose metabolism raises the cytosolic ATP/ADP ratio, which closes ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels, depolarizes the cell, and opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels; the Ca²⁺ influx triggers exocytosis of insulin granules. Insulin then promotes glucose uptake by skeletal muscle and adipose tissue through GLUT4 and suppresses hepatic glucose output. Type 2 diabetes typically begins with insulin resistance, which β cells at first offset by secreting more insulin; diabetes appears when secretion can no longer keep up.\n\nTo compare these stages, investigators gave a 75-g oral glucose load to fasting adults in three groups: controls with normal glucose tolerance, adults with prediabetes, and adults with type 2 diabetes who were not taking glucose-lowering drugs. Venous plasma glucose (mg/dL) and insulin (μU/mL) were measured every 30 min for 2 h and again at 3 h (Figure 1). By standard criteria, a 2-h glucose of 140–199 mg/dL indicates impaired glucose tolerance, and a value of 200 mg/dL or more indicates diabetes. Early β-cell function was estimated with the insulinogenic index, defined as the rise in insulin from 0 to 30 min divided by the rise in glucose over the same interval.\n\nIn a follow-up study, the same control participants received an intravenous glucose infusion adjusted so that their plasma glucose matched the oral-test values at every time point. Under these conditions, plasma insulin at 30 min was only 25 μU/mL.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Plasma glucose (filled markers) and insulin (open markers) after a 75-g oral glucose load","x":{"label":"Time after glucose load (min)","min":0,"max":180,"step":30},"y":{"label":"Glucose (mg/dL) or insulin (μU/mL)","min":0,"max":300,"step":50},"series":[{"name":"Glucose, control","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,90],[30,145],[60,125],[90,105],[120,95],[180,85]]},{"name":"Glucose, prediabetes","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,100],[30,175],[60,185],[90,170],[120,155],[180,115]]},{"name":"Glucose, type 2","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,150],[30,220],[60,270],[90,280],[120,260],[180,210]]},{"name":"Insulin, control","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,8],[30,60],[60,50],[90,35],[120,20],[180,10]]},{"name":"Insulin, prediabetes","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,20],[30,80],[60,120],[90,110],[120,90],[180,40]]},{"name":"Insulin, type 2","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,15],[30,25],[60,40],[90,45],[120,40],[180,30]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"What is the plasma glucose concentration of the prediabetes group 2 h after the glucose load?","choices":["90 mg/dL","155 mg/dL","185 mg/dL","260 mg/dL"],"ans":1,"exp":"2 h = 120 min; the filled ■ prediabetes glucose curve reads 155 mg/dL there. **Match both the series (glucose, not insulin) and the x value (120, not the peak)**.","how":{"ask":"Convert hours to the x-axis units, then pick the correct one of six curves sharing one y-axis.","know":"• Filled markers = glucose in mg/dL; open markers = insulin in μU/mL, plotted on the same numeric axis.\n• 2 h = 120 min.","steps":[{"text":"Glucose and insulin share the y-axis, so identify the series by marker.","hl":"Venous plasma glucose (mg/dL) and insulin (μU/mL) were measured"},{"text":"Read filled ■ prediabetes glucose at 120 min: 155 mg/dL.","graph":{"focus":["Glucose, prediabetes"],"read":[{"series":"Glucose, prediabetes","x":120}],"xLine":120}},"155 mg/dL lies in the impaired-tolerance range of 140–199 mg/dL."],"trap":"90 is the open □ prediabetes insulin curve at 120 min; it is in μU/mL, not glucose. 185 is the glucose peak at 60 min."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"At 60 min, plasma insulin in the prediabetes group is how many times that in the control group?","choices":["0.42","1.5","2.4","3.7"],"ans":2,"exp":"□ prediabetes insulin = 120 μU/mL and ○ control insulin = 50 μU/mL at 60 min; **prediabetes ÷ control = 120/50 = 2.4**.","how":{"ask":"Read two insulin curves at the same time and divide the prediabetes value by the control value.","know":"• 'How many times A is B' means A ÷ B.\n• Insulin curves are the open markers.","steps":[{"text":"Read □ insulin, prediabetes at 60 min: 120 μU/mL.","graph":{"focus":["Insulin, prediabetes","Insulin, control"],"read":[{"series":"Insulin, prediabetes","x":60}],"xLine":60}},{"text":"Read ○ insulin, control at 60 min: 50 μU/mL.","graph":{"focus":["Insulin, prediabetes","Insulin, control"],"read":[{"series":"Insulin, control","x":60}],"xLine":60}},"120 ÷ 50 = 2.4."],"trap":"0.42 divides control by prediabetes; 3.7 uses the filled ■ prediabetes glucose value, 185, instead of insulin."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Which value is closest to the insulinogenic index of the type 2 diabetes group, expressed in μU/mL of insulin per mg/dL of glucose?","choices":["0.14","0.80","0.95","7.0"],"ans":0,"exp":"Δinsulin = 25 − 15 = 10 μU/mL; Δglucose = 220 − 150 = 70 mg/dL; **index = Δinsulin ÷ Δglucose = 10/70 ≈ 0.14**.","how":{"ask":"Turn the passage's word definition into a calculation and pull four numbers from two type 2 curves.","know":"• Insulinogenic index = (insulin₃₀ − insulin₀) ÷ (glucose₃₀ − glucose₀).\n• A low value means the β cells release little insulin early relative to the glucose stimulus.","steps":[{"text":"Definition of the index.","hl":"defined as the rise in insulin from 0 to 30 min divided by the rise in glucose over the same interval"},{"text":"Read △ insulin, type 2 at 0 and 30 min: 15 → 25, a rise of 10 μU/mL.","graph":{"focus":["Insulin, type 2"],"read":[{"series":"Insulin, type 2","x":0},{"series":"Insulin, type 2","x":30}]}},{"text":"Read ▲ glucose, type 2 at 0 and 30 min: 150 → 220, a rise of 70 mg/dL.","graph":{"focus":["Glucose, type 2"],"read":[{"series":"Glucose, type 2","x":0},{"series":"Glucose, type 2","x":30}]}},"10 ÷ 70 ≈ 0.14."],"trap":"7.0 inverts the ratio (glucose rise ÷ insulin rise). 0.80 is the prediabetes index: 60/75."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which conclusion about the prediabetes group is best supported by Figure 1?","choices":["Insulin release is lower, so glucose rises more than in controls","Glucose is absorbed from the gut faster than in controls","Insulin sensitivity is normal, but insulin release is delayed","Insulin release is higher, but each unit lowers glucose less"],"ans":3,"exp":"At 90 min prediabetic insulin is about 3 times control, yet glucose is still higher: **more insulin with less glucose lowering = insulin resistance with compensatory hypersecretion**.","how":{"ask":"Compare both the insulin and glucose curves of the prediabetes group with the control curves at the same time.","know":"• Insulin resistance: target tissues respond less to a given insulin level.\n• β-cell compensation: secretion rises to offset resistance.\n• High glucose with high insulin points to resistance, not secretory failure.","steps":[{"text":"Read □ vs ○ insulin at 90 min: 110 vs 35 μU/mL.","graph":{"focus":["Insulin, prediabetes","Insulin, control"],"read":[{"series":"Insulin, prediabetes","x":90},{"series":"Insulin, control","x":90}],"xLine":90}},{"text":"Read ■ vs ● glucose at 90 min: 170 vs 105 mg/dL.","graph":{"focus":["Glucose, prediabetes","Glucose, control"],"read":[{"series":"Glucose, prediabetes","x":90},{"series":"Glucose, control","x":90}],"xLine":90}},{"text":"This matches the stage the passage describes.","hl":"which β cells at first offset by secreting more insulin"}],"trap":"'Insulin release is lower' describes the type 2 group, whose insulin peaks at only 45 μU/mL; prediabetic insulin is higher than control at every time point."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"Sulfonylurea drugs bind and close β-cell ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels. If the type 2 group took a sulfonylurea before the test, the most likely result would be:","choices":["lower 30-min insulin, because Ca²⁺ entry is blocked","higher 30-min insulin, because β cells depolarize","higher 30-min insulin, because β cells hyperpolarize","lower 30-min glucose, because muscle gains GLUT2"],"ans":1,"exp":"Closing K_ATP channels mimics a high ATP/ADP ratio: **less K⁺ efflux → depolarization → voltage-gated Ca²⁺ entry → insulin exocytosis**.","how":{"ask":"Place the drug's target in the passage's chain of events and follow the chain forward to insulin.","know":"• K⁺ efflux through open channels keeps the membrane polarized.\n• Closing K⁺ channels depolarizes the cell.\n• Depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels; Ca²⁺ triggers exocytosis.","steps":[{"text":"The secretion pathway in the passage.","hl":"closes ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels, depolarizes the cell, and opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels"},{"text":"Read △ insulin, type 2 at 30 min: only 25 μU/mL, so early secretion has room to rise.","graph":{"focus":["Insulin, type 2","Insulin, control"],"read":[{"series":"Insulin, type 2","x":30}]}},"The drug skips the glucose-metabolism step and closes the channels directly → more insulin."],"trap":"'Hyperpolarize' gets the outcome right but the reason wrong: removing an outward K⁺ current makes V_m more positive, not more negative."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"The follow-up study best supports which explanation for the difference in control insulin at 30 min between the oral and intravenous tests?","choices":["Higher plasma glucose in the oral test drove more secretion","Intravenous glucose cannot enter β cells through GLUT2","Gut hormones released by oral glucose amplify secretion","Oral glucose reaches β cells mainly as fructose"],"ans":2,"exp":"Glucose was matched, yet oral insulin (60) was 2.4 times IV insulin (25): **the extra secretion comes from gut-derived incretins such as GLP-1 and GIP**.","how":{"ask":"Notice what was held constant between the two tests and what still differed, then name the factor present only with oral intake.","know":"• Incretin effect: oral glucose triggers intestinal L and K cells to release GLP-1 and GIP, which enhance glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.\n• A matched-glucose design removes glucose level as an explanation.","steps":[{"text":"Plasma glucose was the same in both tests.","hl":"adjusted so that their plasma glucose matched the oral-test values at every time point"},{"text":"Read ○ insulin, control at 30 min in the oral test: 60 μU/mL.","graph":{"focus":["Insulin, control"],"read":[{"series":"Insulin, control","x":30}]}},{"text":"IV value from the passage: 25 μU/mL. 60 ÷ 25 = 2.4-fold more insulin orally.","hl":"plasma insulin at 30 min was only 25 μU/mL"}],"trap":"'Higher plasma glucose in the oral test' is ruled out by design: the IV infusion matched glucose at every time point."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_bio_3","subj":"biology","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biology","title":"Reshaping the Action Potential","passage":"In a neuron, the resting membrane potential (V_m, the potential of the cytoplasm relative to the extracellular fluid) lies near −70 mV because the resting membrane is far more permeable to K⁺ than to Na⁺. When a stimulus depolarizes the membrane to threshold, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, Na⁺ flows in, and V_m moves toward the Na⁺ equilibrium potential, E_Na. The Na⁺ channels then inactivate while delayed voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, and K⁺ efflux repolarizes the membrane, briefly overshooting rest as an afterhyperpolarization.\n\nInvestigators recorded V_m from cultured mammalian neurons at 37 °C in whole-cell current clamp. A brief current pulse ending at t = 1.0 ms brought every cell to threshold at that time. Cells were bathed in normal saline ([Na⁺]o = 145 mM, [K⁺]o = 4 mM; [Na⁺]i = 15 mM, [K⁺]i = 140 mM), in saline containing tetraethylammonium (TEA), a blocker of delayed K⁺ channels, in saline with [Na⁺]o lowered to 72.5 mM by replacement with an impermeant cation, or in saline with [K⁺]o raised to 10 mM. Figure 1 shows a representative recording for each condition.\n\nThe investigators also estimated that about 6 × 10⁶ Na⁺ ions enter one of these neurons during a single control action potential. These ions are later removed by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, which moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in for each ATP hydrolyzed.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Membrane potential of cultured neurons under four bath conditions","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"V_m (mV)","min":-100,"max":60,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Control","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[0.5,-70],[1,-55],[1.5,35],[2,0],[2.5,-50],[3,-80],[3.5,-78],[4,-74],[5,-71],[6,-70],[8,-70]]},{"name":"TEA","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[0.5,-70],[1,-55],[1.5,35],[2,30],[2.5,25],[3,20],[3.5,10],[4,-10],[4.5,-40],[5,-65],[6,-70],[8,-70]]},{"name":"Low Na⁺","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[0.5,-70],[1,-55],[1.5,15],[2,-10],[2.5,-55],[3,-80],[3.5,-78],[4,-74],[5,-71],[6,-70],[8,-70]]},{"name":"High K⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-58],[0.5,-58],[1,-48],[1.5,10],[2,-15],[2.5,-50],[3,-65],[3.5,-62],[4,-60],[5,-58],[6,-58],[8,-58]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"What is the amplitude of the action potential in low-Na⁺ saline, measured from the resting potential to the peak?","choices":["15 mV","70 mV","85 mV","105 mV"],"ans":2,"exp":"▲ rests at −70 mV and peaks at +15 mV; **amplitude = peak − rest = 15 − (−70) = 85 mV**.","how":{"ask":"Find two values on one curve (rest and peak) and take the difference, keeping track of the negative sign.","know":"• Amplitude from rest = V_peak − V_rest.\n• Subtracting a negative number adds its magnitude.","steps":[{"text":"Read ▲ Low Na⁺ at t = 0: resting V_m = −70 mV.","graph":{"focus":["Low Na⁺"],"read":[{"series":"Low Na⁺","x":0}]}},{"text":"Read ▲ Low Na⁺ at its peak, t = 1.5 ms: +15 mV.","graph":{"focus":["Low Na⁺"],"read":[{"series":"Low Na⁺","x":1.5}]}},"15 − (−70) = 85 mV."],"trap":"105 mV is the control amplitude: 35 − (−70). 15 mV is only the overshoot above 0 mV, not the distance from rest."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"How does the change in peak V_m produced by low-Na⁺ saline compare with the change in E_Na predicted by the Nernst equation?","choices":["Peak falls 20 mV; E_Na falls about 61 mV","Peak falls 35 mV; E_Na falls about 18 mV","Peak falls 20 mV; E_Na falls about 30 mV","Peak falls 20 mV; E_Na falls about 18 mV"],"ans":3,"exp":"Peak drops from +35 to +15 mV. **E = (61.5 mV) log([Na⁺]o/[Na⁺]i)**: +60.6 mV normally, +42.1 mV at 72.5 mM, a fall of 61.5·log 2 ≈ 18 mV, so the peak tracks E_Na.","how":{"ask":"Measure the peak shift between two curves, then compute how much halving [Na⁺]o moves E_Na.","know":"• Nernst at 37 °C for a +1 ion: E = 61.5 mV × log(C_out/C_in).\n• Halving C_out changes E by 61.5 × log 2 ≈ 18.5 mV, regardless of the starting value.\n• The AP peak approaches but stays below E_Na.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● Control and ▲ Low Na⁺ at t = 1.5 ms: +35 and +15 mV, a 20-mV fall.","graph":{"focus":["Control","Low Na⁺"],"read":[{"series":"Control","x":1.5},{"series":"Low Na⁺","x":1.5}],"xLine":1.5}},{"text":"The new external Na⁺ is half of normal.","hl":"[Na⁺]o lowered to 72.5 mM"},"Normal E_Na = 61.5 log(145/15) ≈ +61 mV; low-Na E_Na = 61.5 log(72.5/15) ≈ +42 mV.","Change ≈ 18 mV, close to the observed 20 mV."],"trap":"'About 30 mV' assumes halving the concentration halves E_Na; E depends on the log of the ratio. 'About 61 mV' is the E_Na value itself, not its change."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"At t = 3.0 ms, how much more positive is V_m in the TEA-treated cell than in the control cell?","choices":["20 mV","50 mV","70 mV","100 mV"],"ans":3,"exp":"At 3.0 ms ■ TEA = +20 mV and ● control = −80 mV; **difference = 20 − (−80) = 100 mV**.","how":{"ask":"Read two curves at one time and subtract, keeping signs.","know":"• 'How much more positive is A than B' = V_A − V_B.\n• TEA blocks delayed K⁺ channels, so repolarization is slowed.","steps":[{"text":"TEA blocks the K⁺ channels that repolarize the cell.","hl":"tetraethylammonium (TEA), a blocker of delayed K⁺ channels"},{"text":"Read ■ TEA at t = 3.0 ms: +20 mV.","graph":{"focus":["TEA","Control"],"read":[{"series":"TEA","x":3}],"xLine":3}},{"text":"Read ● Control at t = 3.0 ms: −80 mV.","graph":{"focus":["TEA","Control"],"read":[{"series":"Control","x":3}],"xLine":3}},"20 − (−80) = 100 mV."],"trap":"70 mV subtracts the control value at 2.5 ms, −50 mV, the wrong x. 20 mV is the TEA reading alone."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"In 10 mM [K⁺]o the action potential peak is lower than in control, although [Na⁺]o is normal. Which explanation is best supported?","choices":["Higher [K⁺]o makes E_Na less positive","The depolarized rest inactivates some Na⁺ channels","The depolarized rest increases the Na⁺ driving force","Higher [K⁺]o blocks the delayed K⁺ channels"],"ans":1,"exp":"Raising [K⁺]o depolarizes rest to −58 mV; **sustained depolarization inactivates a fraction of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels**, so fewer are available and the peak drops to +10 mV.","how":{"ask":"Explain a smaller peak when E_Na itself has not changed, using what the High K⁺ curve shows at rest.","know":"• E_Na depends only on Na⁺ concentrations.\n• Na⁺ channels inactivate at depolarized potentials and need hyperpolarization to recover.\n• Fewer available Na⁺ channels → smaller Na⁺ current → lower peak.","steps":[{"text":"Only [K⁺]o was changed.","hl":"saline with [K⁺]o raised to 10 mM"},{"text":"Read ◆ High K⁺ at t = 0: rest is −58 mV, 12 mV depolarized versus ● −70 mV.","graph":{"focus":["High K⁺","Control"],"read":[{"series":"High K⁺","x":0},{"series":"Control","x":0}]}},{"text":"Read ◆ High K⁺ at t = 1.5 ms: peak +10 mV versus ● +35 mV.","graph":{"focus":["High K⁺","Control"],"read":[{"series":"High K⁺","x":1.5},{"series":"Control","x":1.5}],"xLine":1.5}}],"trap":"'Higher [K⁺]o makes E_Na less positive' is wrong: the Na⁺ gradient is unchanged. 'Increases the Na⁺ driving force' is true but would raise, not lower, the peak."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"A second identical current pulse delivered to the control cell at t = 2.0 ms fails to evoke another action potential. The best explanation is that at this time:","choices":["most voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are inactivated","the membrane is hyperpolarized below rest","the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase has stopped operating","Na⁺ has reached equilibrium across the membrane"],"ans":0,"exp":"At 2.0 ms the control cell is at 0 mV during repolarization; **the absolute refractory period is due to Na⁺-channel inactivation**, not hyperpolarization.","how":{"ask":"Check the membrane state at that time on the graph, then name the property that prevents a new spike.","know":"• Absolute refractory period: inactivated Na⁺ channels cannot reopen until the membrane repolarizes.\n• Relative refractory period: during the afterhyperpolarization, a larger stimulus can fire the cell.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● Control at t = 2.0 ms: 0 mV, still depolarized.","graph":{"focus":["Control"],"read":[{"series":"Control","x":2}],"xLine":2}},{"text":"The passage states what happens to Na⁺ channels after the upstroke.","hl":"The Na⁺ channels then inactivate"},"Inactivated channels cannot open → no second spike."],"trap":"'Hyperpolarized below rest' describes 3.0 ms, the afterhyperpolarization, not 2.0 ms when V_m is 0 mV."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"To restore the Na⁺ gradient after one control action potential, how many ATP must the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase hydrolyze, and how many K⁺ does it bring in while doing so?","choices":["6 × 10⁶ ATP; 4 × 10⁶ K⁺","3 × 10⁶ ATP; 2 × 10⁶ K⁺","2 × 10⁶ ATP; 4 × 10⁶ K⁺","2 × 10⁶ ATP; 6 × 10⁶ K⁺"],"ans":2,"exp":"6 × 10⁶ Na⁺ ÷ 3 Na⁺ per ATP = 2 × 10⁶ ATP; **2 K⁺ per ATP** → 4 × 10⁶ K⁺ imported.","how":{"ask":"Use the pump stoichiometry to convert the number of Na⁺ ions into ATP consumed and K⁺ moved.","know":"• Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in, 1 ATP → ADP + Pᵢ per cycle.\n• The pump is electrogenic: net one positive charge out per cycle.","steps":[{"text":"Na⁺ that entered.","hl":"about 6 × 10⁶ Na⁺ ions enter"},{"text":"Stoichiometry of the pump.","hl":"moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in for each ATP hydrolyzed"},"ATP = 6 × 10⁶ ÷ 3 = 2 × 10⁶.","K⁺ = 2 × (2 × 10⁶) = 4 × 10⁶."],"trap":"'2 × 10⁶ ATP; 6 × 10⁶ K⁺' gets the ATP right but assumes a 1:1 Na⁺:K⁺ exchange; the pump moves only 2 K⁺ per 3 Na⁺."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_bio_4","subj":"biology","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Biology","title":"Knockdown and Rescue of SREBP2","passage":"Cells take up cholesterol from plasma low-density lipoprotein (LDL) through the LDL receptor (LDLR). Transcription of the LDLR gene is controlled by sterol regulatory element–binding protein 2 (SREBP2), which is made as an inactive precursor in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane bound to the escort protein SCAP. When ER cholesterol is low, SCAP carries SREBP2 to the Golgi, where two proteases, S1P and then S2P, cut it in sequence. The released N-terminal fragment enters the nucleus and activates genes containing sterol regulatory elements, including LDLR. When ER cholesterol is high, SCAP binds the ER protein INSIG, and SREBP2 stays in the ER uncleaved.\n\nTo test whether SREBP2 is required for LDLR expression, researchers transfected cultured human liver cells with a small interfering RNA (siRNA) targeting SREBP2 mRNA or with a scrambled siRNA that matches no human mRNA. Some siRNA-treated cells were also given a plasmid encoding SREBP2 with silent mutations that make its mRNA resistant to the siRNA, or the same plasmid lacking the SREBP2 insert (empty vector). Cells were then grown for 16 h in sterol-depleted medium or in sterol-replete medium containing cholesterol and 25-hydroxycholesterol. LDLR mRNA was measured by quantitative RT-PCR and expressed as a percentage of the level in untreated cells in sterol-depleted medium (Figure 1; mean ± SD, n = 4).\n\nWestern blots confirmed that the siRNA lowered SREBP2 precursor protein by more than 80% and that the resistant plasmid restored it to near-normal levels.","figure":{"type":"bar","caption":"Figure 1  LDLR mRNA in liver cells after SREBP2 knockdown and rescue, in sterol-depleted and sterol-replete medium","x":{"label":"Treatment"},"y":{"label":"LDLR mRNA (% of untreated, sterol-depleted)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"categories":["Untreated","Scrambled siRNA","SREBP2 siRNA","siRNA + empty vector","siRNA + resistant SREBP2"],"series":[{"name":"Sterol-depleted","values":[100,96,22,20,88],"errors":[8,7,4,5,9]},{"name":"Sterol-replete","values":[30,29,12,13,27],"errors":[4,5,3,3,4]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"In sterol-depleted medium, by about what percentage did SREBP2 siRNA lower LDLR mRNA relative to scrambled siRNA?","choices":["23%","45%","77%","96%"],"ans":2,"exp":"Scrambled = 96%, SREBP2 siRNA = 22%; **percent decrease = (96 − 22)/96 ≈ 77%**.","how":{"ask":"Compute a percent decrease using the matched control as the reference, not the remaining level.","know":"• Percent decrease = (reference − treated) ÷ reference × 100.\n• The proper reference for an siRNA is the scrambled siRNA, which received the same transfection.","steps":[{"text":"Read the scrambled siRNA bar in sterol-depleted medium: 96%.","graph":{"bars":[["Scrambled siRNA","Sterol-depleted"]],"focus":["Sterol-depleted"]}},{"text":"Read the SREBP2 siRNA bar in sterol-depleted medium: 22%.","graph":{"bars":[["SREBP2 siRNA","Sterol-depleted"]],"focus":["Sterol-depleted"]}},"(96 − 22) ÷ 96 = 74 ÷ 96 ≈ 0.77 → 77% decrease."],"trap":"23% is the amount remaining, 22 ÷ 96, not the amount lost."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"In cells given scrambled siRNA, how many times higher is LDLR mRNA in sterol-depleted medium than in sterol-replete medium?","choices":["0.30","1.8","3.3","67"],"ans":2,"exp":"Depleted 96% ÷ replete 29% ≈ 3.3; **fold induction = depleted ÷ replete**.","how":{"ask":"Divide two bars in the same treatment group, in the order the question names them.","know":"• 'How many times higher is A than B' = A ÷ B.\n• A difference such as 96 − 29 is not a fold change.","steps":[{"text":"Read scrambled siRNA, sterol-depleted: 96%.","graph":{"bars":[["Scrambled siRNA","Sterol-depleted"]]}},{"text":"Read scrambled siRNA, sterol-replete: 29%.","graph":{"bars":[["Scrambled siRNA","Sterol-replete"]]}},"96 ÷ 29 ≈ 3.3."],"trap":"0.30 divides the wrong way; 1.8 is the fold change in the SREBP2 siRNA group, 22 ÷ 12; 67 is a difference."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which comparison best rules out the possibility that the drop in LDLR mRNA was caused by the SREBP2 siRNA silencing some other, unintended mRNA?","choices":["SREBP2 siRNA versus scrambled siRNA","Resistant SREBP2 versus empty vector, both with siRNA","Scrambled siRNA versus untreated cells","Sterol-depleted versus sterol-replete medium"],"ans":1,"exp":"If restoring only SREBP2, with the siRNA still present, brings LDLR back from 20% to 88%, the loss was due to SREBP2. **A rescue with a resistant cDNA controls for off-target effects**.","how":{"ask":"Find the condition that keeps the siRNA in the cell but gives back only the intended target.","know":"• Off-target effect: an siRNA partly matches and silences an unintended mRNA.\n• Scrambled siRNA controls for transfection and nonspecific RNAi effects, not for the specific sequence.\n• Rescue: re-express the target in a form the siRNA cannot hit; if the phenotype reverses, the target was responsible.","steps":[{"text":"The rescue construct is immune to the siRNA.","hl":"silent mutations that make its mRNA resistant to the siRNA"},{"text":"Compare sterol-depleted bars: siRNA + resistant SREBP2 = 88% versus siRNA + empty vector = 20%.","graph":{"bars":[["siRNA + resistant SREBP2","Sterol-depleted"],["siRNA + empty vector","Sterol-depleted"]],"focus":["Sterol-depleted"]}},"Both groups carry the same siRNA, so any off-target silencing is equal; only SREBP2 differs."],"trap":"'SREBP2 siRNA versus scrambled siRNA' shows the effect is sequence-specific but cannot tell whether the sequence hit SREBP2 or another mRNA."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Based only on the data in Figure 1, which conclusion is justified?","choices":["SREBP2 knockdown lowers the rate of LDL uptake","SREBP2 binds directly to the LDLR promoter","SREBP2 knockdown lowers surface LDLR protein","SREBP2 is required for normal LDLR mRNA in low sterol"],"ans":3,"exp":"The y-axis measures **LDLR mRNA**, so the data support a conclusion about transcript levels only, not protein, LDL uptake, or direct DNA binding.","how":{"ask":"Check exactly what quantity the y-axis reports and reject conclusions about anything that was not measured.","know":"• qRT-PCR measures mRNA.\n• Protein level, receptor function and promoter binding need other assays: Western blot, LDL uptake assay, ChIP.","steps":[{"text":"What was measured.","hl":"LDLR mRNA was measured by quantitative RT-PCR"},{"text":"Sterol-depleted: SREBP2 siRNA 22% vs scrambled 96%, restored to 88% by rescue.","graph":{"bars":[["SREBP2 siRNA","Sterol-depleted"],["Scrambled siRNA","Sterol-depleted"],["siRNA + resistant SREBP2","Sterol-depleted"]],"focus":["Sterol-depleted"]}},"Only a statement about LDLR mRNA follows directly."],"trap":"'Lowers surface LDLR protein' is likely true biologically but was not measured; the Western blots in the passage measured SREBP2, not LDLR."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. Statins lower plasma LDL mainly because hepatocytes:","choices":["cleave more SREBP2 and make more LDLR","bind more INSIG and make less LDLR","secrete more LDLR into the plasma","convert LDL directly into bile acids"],"ans":0,"exp":"Less synthesis lowers ER cholesterol, so **SCAP escorts SREBP2 to the Golgi for cleavage, LDLR transcription rises, and the liver clears more LDL** from plasma.","how":{"ask":"Treat a statin as a way of lowering ER cholesterol, then follow the passage's pathway to LDLR.","know":"• HMG-CoA reductase converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate, an early step of cholesterol synthesis.\n• Low ER cholesterol → SCAP–SREBP2 to Golgi → S1P, S2P cleavage → nuclear SREBP2 → LDLR transcription.\n• More hepatic LDLR → more LDL removed from blood.","steps":[{"text":"Low ER cholesterol activates the pathway.","hl":"When ER cholesterol is low, SCAP carries SREBP2 to the Golgi"},{"text":"Untreated cells: LDLR mRNA is 100% with low sterol versus 30% with high sterol.","graph":{"bars":[["Untreated","Sterol-depleted"],["Untreated","Sterol-replete"]]}},"A statin moves cells toward the low-sterol state → more LDLR → lower plasma LDL."],"trap":"'Bind more INSIG' is the high-cholesterol state; statins lower cholesterol, which releases SCAP from INSIG."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Suppose siRNA-treated cells were instead given a resistant plasmid encoding only the nuclear N-terminal fragment of SREBP2. In sterol-replete medium, LDLR mRNA would be closest to:","choices":["88%","27%","12%","3%"],"ans":0,"exp":"The N-terminal fragment does not need SCAP transport or S1P/S2P cleavage, so **sterols can no longer hold it in the ER via INSIG**; LDLR stays near the sterol-depleted rescue level, about 88%.","how":{"ask":"Decide which step of sterol regulation the new construct bypasses, then pick the bar that represents an unregulated, active SREBP2.","know":"• Sterols act on the precursor: INSIG retains SCAP–SREBP2 in the ER.\n• The cleaved N-terminal fragment is the active transcription factor.\n• A construct that makes the fragment directly escapes sterol control.","steps":[{"text":"Sterols block activation at the ER step.","hl":"When ER cholesterol is high, SCAP binds the ER protein INSIG, and SREBP2 stays in the ER uncleaved."},{"text":"Full-length rescue: 88% with low sterol but 27% with high sterol, because it is still sterol-regulated.","graph":{"bars":[["siRNA + resistant SREBP2","Sterol-depleted"],["siRNA + resistant SREBP2","Sterol-replete"]]}},"The fragment skips the regulated step → behaves like the low-sterol rescue, about 88%."],"trap":"27% is what the full-length rescue gives in sterol-replete medium; it assumes the fragment is still retained by INSIG."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_chemphys_1","subj":"gen_chem","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Chem/Phys","title":"Titration of Lactic Acid and HCl","passage":"Lactic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH, is produced by anaerobic glycolysis in skeletal muscle. During intense exercise it accumulates and is released into the blood, where the acid load is buffered chiefly by the bicarbonate system. Because lactic acid is a weak monoprotic acid, its titration curve differs markedly from that of a strong acid such as HCl, the acid secreted by gastric parietal cells.\n\nIn an experiment, a student placed 25.0 mL of a lactic acid solution of unknown concentration in one flask and 25.0 mL of an HCl solution of unknown concentration in a second flask. Each sample was titrated with 0.100 M NaOH, and the pH was recorded with a calibrated glass electrode after each addition (Figure 1). The equivalence point of each titration was taken as the volume at the midpoint of the steepest rise in pH.\n\nBefore the equivalence point, each mole of NaOH added to the lactic acid flask converts one mole of lactic acid into lactate (Reaction 1), so the flask contains a mixture of a weak acid and its conjugate base.\n\nReaction 1: CH₃CH(OH)COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃CH(OH)COO⁻ + H₂O\n\nThe student proposed that the portion of the lactic acid curve where pH changes most slowly identifies the composition that would make the best buffer for a later enzyme assay.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Titration of 25.0 mL of lactic acid and 25.0 mL of HCl with 0.100 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of 0.100 M NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":40,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Lactic acid","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.49],[2,2.97],[4,3.28],[6,3.5],[8,3.69],[10,3.86],[12,4.04],[14,4.23],[15,4.34],[16,4.46],[18,4.82],[19,5.14],[20,8.25],[21,11.34],[22,11.63],[24,11.91],[26,12.07],[28,12.18],[29,12.22],[30,12.26],[31,12.29],[32,12.32],[34,12.38],[36,12.42],[38,12.46],[40,12.49]]},{"name":"HCl","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0.92],[2,0.98],[4,1.05],[6,1.11],[8,1.18],[10,1.24],[12,1.31],[14,1.39],[15,1.43],[16,1.47],[18,1.55],[19,1.6],[20,1.65],[21,1.71],[22,1.77],[24,1.91],[26,2.11],[28,2.42],[29,2.73],[30,7],[31,11.25],[32,11.55],[34,11.83],[36,11.99],[38,12.1],[40,12.19]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Based on Figure 1, the pKa of lactic acid is closest to:","choices":["2.5","3.9","5.1","8.3"],"ans":1,"exp":"At the half-equivalence point [lactate] = [lactic acid], so **pH = pKa**. Equivalence is at 20.0 mL, so read the ● curve at 10.0 mL: pH 3.86.","how":{"ask":"Find the point on the lactic acid curve where the pH equals the pKa.","know":"• Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]).\n• When half the acid has been neutralized, [A⁻] = [HA], the log term is 0, and pH = pKa.\n• The half-equivalence volume is half the equivalence volume.","steps":[{"text":"Find the lactic acid equivalence point: the steep jump on ● is centered at 20.0 mL (pH 8.25).","hl":"the midpoint of the steepest rise in pH","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":20}]}},{"text":"Half-equivalence volume = 20.0 ÷ 2 = 10.0 mL.","hl":"converts one mole of lactic acid into lactate","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"xLine":10}},{"text":"Read ● at 10.0 mL: pH 3.86, so pKa ≈ 3.9.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":10}]}}],"trap":"8.3 is the pH at the equivalence point, where the solution is mostly lactate; 2.5 is the starting pH of the acid, which depends on concentration, not just pKa."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"What was the concentration of the HCl solution?","choices":["0.083 M","0.100 M","0.120 M","0.150 M"],"ans":2,"exp":"The ■ curve jumps at 30.0 mL. **Moles OH⁻ = moles H⁺ at equivalence**: 0.0300 L × 0.100 M = 3.00 mmol; 3.00 mmol ÷ 25.0 mL = 0.120 M.","how":{"ask":"Use the equivalence volume of the HCl curve to find the moles of acid, then its molarity.","know":"• At the equivalence point of a monoprotic acid, mol NaOH added = mol acid originally present.\n• M_acid × V_acid = M_base × V_base(eq).","steps":[{"text":"Read ■ just before and after the jump: 2.73 at 29.0 mL and 11.25 at 31.0 mL, so equivalence is at 30.0 mL (pH 7.00).","graph":{"focus":["HCl"],"read":[{"series":"HCl","x":29},{"series":"HCl","x":31},{"series":"HCl","x":30}]}},{"text":"mol NaOH = 0.0300 L × 0.100 M = 3.00 × 10⁻³ mol = mol HCl.","hl":"titrated with 0.100 M NaOH"},{"text":"[HCl] = 3.00 × 10⁻³ mol ÷ 0.0250 L = 0.120 M.","hl":"25.0 mL of an HCl solution of unknown concentration"}],"trap":"0.083 M comes from dividing the wrong way, 25.0 × 0.100 ÷ 30.0. The HCl needed more base than its own volume, so it must be more concentrated than 0.100 M."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"After 15.0 mL of NaOH has been added to the lactic acid flask, the ratio of lactate to lactic acid in the flask is closest to:","choices":["0.33","0.75","1.5","3.0"],"ans":3,"exp":"Equivalence at 20.0 mL means 2.00 mmol lactic acid. **Each mol OH⁻ converts one mol HA to A⁻**: 1.50 mmol A⁻ and 0.50 mmol HA, ratio 3.0. Check: pH 4.34 = 3.86 + log 3.","how":{"ask":"Track how much of the original acid has been converted by 15.0 mL of base.","know":"• Reaction 1 is 1:1, so mol lactate formed = mol OH⁻ added before equivalence.\n• mol lactic acid left = mol initially present − mol OH⁻ added.","steps":[{"text":"Equivalence at 20.0 mL → total lactic acid = 0.0200 L × 0.100 M = 2.00 mmol.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":20}]}},{"text":"15.0 mL base = 1.50 mmol OH⁻ → 1.50 mmol lactate, 0.50 mmol lactic acid left; ratio = 1.50 ÷ 0.50 = 3.0.","hl":"each mole of NaOH added to the lactic acid flask converts one mole of lactic acid into lactate"},{"text":"Check on the graph: ● at 15.0 mL reads pH 4.34; 4.34 − 3.86 = 0.48 = log 3.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":15},{"series":"Lactic acid","x":10}]}}],"trap":"0.33 is the right numbers divided the wrong way, acid over base; 0.75 compares base added to total acid, 15 ÷ 20, which is the fraction converted, not the ratio."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Based on Figure 1, over which range of added NaOH does the lactic acid solution act most effectively as a buffer?","choices":["0 to 4 mL","6 to 14 mL","16 to 20 mL","20 to 24 mL"],"ans":1,"exp":"A buffer resists pH change, so look for the **flattest part of the curve centered on the pKa**. From 6 to 14 mL the pH rises only 0.73 over 8 mL.","how":{"ask":"Find where adding base changes the pH least, per mL added.","know":"• A buffer contains comparable amounts of a weak acid and its conjugate base.\n• Buffering is strongest within about ±1 pH unit of the pKa, near half-equivalence.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● at 6 and 14 mL: 3.50 → 4.23, ΔpH = 0.73 over 8 mL.","hl":"the portion of the lactic acid curve where pH changes most slowly","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":6},{"series":"Lactic acid","x":14}]}},{"text":"Read ● at 16 and 20 mL: 4.46 → 8.25, ΔpH = 3.8 over 4 mL; at 20 and 24 mL: 8.25 → 11.91.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":16},{"series":"Lactic acid","x":20},{"series":"Lactic acid","x":24}]}},{"text":"Read ● at 0 and 4 mL: 2.49 → 3.28, ΔpH = 0.79 over only 4 mL, about twice the slope of the 6–14 mL region.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":0},{"series":"Lactic acid","x":4}]}}],"trap":"0 to 4 mL looks flat at a glance, but little lactate is present yet, so the pH changes about twice as fast per mL as near the half-equivalence point."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"In arterial blood at normal physiological pH, the ratio of lactate to lactic acid is closest to:","choices":["2.9 × 10⁻⁴","1.9","3.5 × 10³","2.5 × 10⁷"],"ans":2,"exp":"Normal blood pH is **7.4**. Henderson–Hasselbalch with the pKa from Figure 1: log([A⁻]/[HA]) = 7.40 − 3.86 = 3.54, so the ratio ≈ 3.5 × 10³.","how":{"ask":"Combine the pKa you read from the figure with a pH you are expected to know.","know":"• Normal arterial blood pH is 7.35–7.45, about 7.4.\n• log([A⁻]/[HA]) = pH − pKa; when pH > pKa, the base form dominates.","steps":[{"text":"Read the pKa: ● at the half-equivalence volume of 10.0 mL gives 3.86.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":10}]}},{"text":"Blood is held near pH 7.40.","hl":"buffered chiefly by the bicarbonate system"},{"text":"log ratio = 7.40 − 3.86 = 3.54 → ratio = 10^3.54 ≈ 3.5 × 10³; lactic acid is almost entirely ionized in blood."}],"trap":"2.9 × 10⁻⁴ is the same calculation inverted, acid over base; it would mean lactic acid is un-ionized in blood, which is false because pH is far above the pKa."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which statement best explains why the pH at the equivalence point differs between the two titrations?","choices":["Lactate is a weak base that reacts with water","Lactic acid was at a higher concentration","Chloride is a weak base that reacts with water","Lactic acid releases two protons per molecule"],"ans":0,"exp":"At equivalence the ● flask holds lactate, the **conjugate base of a weak acid**, which hydrolyzes to make OH⁻ (pH 8.25). Cl⁻ is the conjugate base of a strong acid and is neutral (pH 7.00).","how":{"ask":"Ask what species is left in each flask at its equivalence point and how it affects pH.","know":"• Conjugate base of a weak acid: A⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HA + OH⁻ → basic solution.\n• Conjugate base of a strong acid, like Cl⁻, does not react appreciably with water.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● at its equivalence point, 20.0 mL: pH 8.25.","graph":{"focus":["Lactic acid"],"read":[{"series":"Lactic acid","x":20}]}},{"text":"Read ■ at its equivalence point, 30.0 mL: pH 7.00.","graph":{"focus":["HCl"],"read":[{"series":"HCl","x":30}]}},{"text":"At equivalence, Reaction 1 is complete: the flask is a lactate solution, and lactate pulls H⁺ from water.","hl":"CH₃CH(OH)COO⁻"}],"trap":"Lactic acid was actually less concentrated, 0.080 M vs 0.120 M, and concentration does not explain a basic equivalence point; one jump on the curve also shows it is monoprotic."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_chemphys_2","subj":"gen_chem","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Chem/Phys","title":"Kinetics of Aspirin Hydrolysis","passage":"Acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin) slowly hydrolyzes in water to salicylic acid and acetic acid (Reaction 1). Because water is present in vast excess, the rate depends measurably only on the aspirin concentration, so the order with respect to aspirin can be found from how [aspirin] changes with time. Hydrolysis matters clinically: liquid aspirin preparations lose potency during storage, and salicylic acid irritates the gastric mucosa.\n\nReaction 1: aspirin + H₂O → salicylic acid + acetic acid\n\nResearchers prepared aspirin solutions in pH 7.4 phosphate buffer and held them at 25 °C or 37 °C. At intervals, an aliquot was removed and the salicylic acid it contained was quantified by adding Fe³⁺, which forms a violet complex with the phenol group of salicylate but not with intact aspirin. The aspirin remaining was calculated as the initial aspirin concentration minus the salicylic acid formed. Run 1 and Run 2 began with 8.0 mM aspirin at 37 °C and 25 °C, respectively; Run 3 began with 4.0 mM aspirin at 37 °C. Figure 1 plots the natural logarithm of the aspirin concentration, expressed in mM, against time.\n\nThe researchers used the rate constants from Runs 1 and 2 to estimate the activation energy of hydrolysis with the Arrhenius equation, k = A e^(−Ea/RT), and concluded that refrigeration would substantially extend the shelf life of a liquid formulation.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Natural log of aspirin concentration versus time during hydrolysis at pH 7.4","x":{"label":"Time (h)","min":0,"max":40,"step":5},"y":{"label":"ln([aspirin] / mM)","min":-1.5,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,2.08],[5,1.73],[10,1.39],[15,1.04],[20,0.69],[25,0.35],[30,0],[35,-0.35],[40,-0.69]]},{"name":"Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,2.08],[5,1.99],[10,1.91],[15,1.82],[20,1.73],[25,1.65],[30,1.56],[35,1.47],[40,1.39]]},{"name":"Run 3: 37 °C, 4.0 mM","marker":"triangle-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,1.39],[5,1.04],[10,0.69],[15,0.35],[20,0],[25,-0.35],[30,-0.69],[35,-1.04],[40,-1.39]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"What is the half-life of aspirin hydrolysis under the conditions of Run 1?","choices":["5 h","10 h","20 h","40 h"],"ans":1,"exp":"Halving [A] lowers ln[A] by **ln 2 = 0.69**. Run 1 falls from 2.08 at t = 0 to 1.39 at t = 10 h, so t½ = 10 h.","how":{"ask":"Translate 'half the aspirin is gone' into a change on this log axis, then find when Run 1 shows that change.","know":"• ln(c/2) = ln c − ln 2 = ln c − 0.69.\n• On an ln[A] vs t plot, one half-life = the time for y to drop by 0.69.\n• For first order, t½ = 0.693/k.","steps":[{"text":"The y-axis is ln[aspirin], not [aspirin].","hl":"Figure 1 plots the natural logarithm of the aspirin concentration","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM"]}},{"text":"Read ● at t = 0: 2.08 = ln 8.0. Half of 8.0 mM is 4.0 mM, ln 4.0 = 1.39.","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":0}],"yLine":1.39}},{"text":"Read ● at t = 10 h: 1.39 → t½ = 10 h.","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":10}]}}],"trap":"40 h is the half-life read from Run 2, the 25 °C line; 20 h is when ln[A] has dropped by 1.39, which is two half-lives."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"The rate constant for Run 1 is how many times the rate constant for Run 2?","choices":["0.25","2.0","4.0","12"],"ans":2,"exp":"For first order, **k = −slope** of ln[A] vs t, and k = 0.693/t½. t½ is 10 h in Run 1 and 40 h in Run 2, so k₁/k₂ = 40/10 = 4.0.","how":{"ask":"Compare how steeply the two 8.0 mM lines fall.","know":"• ln[A] = ln[A]₀ − kt, so the slope is −k.\n• A shorter half-life means a larger k: k₁/k₂ = t½,₂ / t½,₁.","steps":[{"text":"Run 1: ● drops from 2.08 to 1.39 by 10 h → t½ = 10 h.","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":0},{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":10}]}},{"text":"Run 2: ■ drops from 2.08 to 1.39 by 40 h → t½ = 40 h.","graph":{"focus":["Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM","x":40}]}},{"text":"k₁/k₂ = 40 h ÷ 10 h = 4.0, the 37 °C reaction is faster.","hl":"Run 1 and Run 2 began with 8.0 mM aspirin at 37 °C and 25 °C"}],"trap":"0.25 is the ratio of half-lives taken the wrong way, 10 ÷ 40; rate constant and half-life are inversely related. 12 is the temperature difference in kelvins."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"What concentration of aspirin remained after 20 h in Run 2?","choices":["1.7 mM","2.4 mM","4.0 mM","5.6 mM"],"ans":3,"exp":"Read ■ at 20 h: 1.73. That is **ln[aspirin]**, so [aspirin] = e^1.73 ≈ 5.6 mM, consistent with half of one 40 h half-life having passed.","how":{"ask":"Read the Run 2 line at 20 h, then remember what quantity the y-axis actually reports.","know":"• y = ln([aspirin]/mM), so [aspirin] = e^y mM.\n• e^1.73 ≈ 5.6; check: after half a half-life, fraction left = 2^(−0.5) = 0.71, and 0.71 × 8.0 = 5.7 mM.","steps":[{"text":"Read ■ at 20 h: y = 1.73.","graph":{"focus":["Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM","x":20}]}},{"text":"The y value is a natural log, not a concentration.","hl":"natural logarithm of the aspirin concentration, expressed in mM"},{"text":"[aspirin] = e^1.73 ≈ 5.6 mM."}],"trap":"1.7 mM is the y-value read as if it were a concentration; the axis is ln[aspirin]. 2.4 mM is the salicylic acid formed, 8.0 − 5.6."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"In Run 1, what concentration of salicylic acid was present at 30 h?","choices":["0 mM","1.0 mM","7.0 mM","8.0 mM"],"ans":2,"exp":"At 30 h ● reads 0.00, so [aspirin] = e⁰ = 1.0 mM. **Each aspirin hydrolyzed gives one salicylic acid**, so salicylic acid = 8.0 − 1.0 = 7.0 mM.","how":{"ask":"Find the aspirin left, then use the reaction stoichiometry to get the product formed.","know":"• Reaction 1 is 1:1: [salicylic acid] formed = [aspirin]₀ − [aspirin]ₜ.\n• ln[A] = 0 means [A] = 1 mM, not zero.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● at 30 h: y = 0.00 → [aspirin] = e⁰ = 1.0 mM.","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":30}]}},{"text":"Aspirin consumed = 8.0 − 1.0 = 7.0 mM.","hl":"The aspirin remaining was calculated as the initial aspirin concentration minus the salicylic acid formed"},{"text":"Reaction 1 is 1:1, so salicylic acid = 7.0 mM.","hl":"aspirin + H₂O → salicylic acid + acetic acid"}],"trap":"1.0 mM is the aspirin remaining, not the product formed; 0 mM treats ln[A] = 0 as no aspirin at all."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which conclusion is best supported by comparing Run 3 with Run 1?","choices":["The reaction is first order, since t½ is independent of initial concentration","The reaction is second order, since Run 3 starts at a lower value","The reaction is zero order, since Run 3 reaches a given ln value sooner","The rate constant doubles when the initial concentration is halved"],"ans":0,"exp":"Run 3 falls from 1.39 to 0.69 in 10 h, the **same slope and half-life** as Run 1. Linear ln[A] vs t with a slope independent of [A]₀ is the signature of first order.","how":{"ask":"Decide what the shape and slope of the Run 3 line say compared with Run 1.","know":"• First order: ln[A] vs t is linear, t½ = 0.693/k does not depend on [A]₀.\n• Second order: 1/[A] vs t is linear, t½ = 1/(k[A]₀) grows as [A]₀ falls.\n• Zero order: [A] vs t is linear.","steps":[{"text":"Run 3 began at half the concentration of Run 1, at the same temperature.","hl":"Run 3 began with 4.0 mM aspirin at 37 °C","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","Run 3: 37 °C, 4.0 mM"]}},{"text":"Read △ at 0 and 10 h: 1.39 → 0.69, a drop of 0.69 in 10 h.","graph":{"focus":["Run 3: 37 °C, 4.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 3: 37 °C, 4.0 mM","x":0},{"series":"Run 3: 37 °C, 4.0 mM","x":10}]}},{"text":"Read ● at 0 and 10 h: 2.08 → 1.39, also 0.69 in 10 h: parallel lines, same k, same t½.","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","Run 3: 37 °C, 4.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":0},{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":10}]}}],"trap":"A lower starting point is just ln 4 vs ln 8; it says nothing about order. If the reaction were second order, the 4.0 mM run would have a longer half-life and a different-shaped plot."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"Which statement best explains why aspirin hydrolyzes faster in Run 1 than in Run 2?","choices":["A larger fraction of collisions have energy of at least Ea","Raising the temperature lowers the activation energy","Raising the temperature shifts equilibrium toward products","Raising the temperature raises the concentration of water"],"ans":0,"exp":"In the Arrhenius equation, Ea is a constant of the reaction; **higher T increases e^(−Ea/RT)**, the fraction of collisions energetic enough to react, so k rises.","how":{"ask":"Identify what temperature changes in the Arrhenius equation and what it leaves constant.","know":"• k = A e^(−Ea/RT): as T rises, −Ea/RT becomes less negative, so k grows.\n• Catalysts lower Ea; temperature does not.\n• Kinetics, the rate, is separate from thermodynamics, the position of equilibrium.","steps":[{"text":"Run 1 is the steeper line: by 20 h ● reads 0.69 while ■ still reads 1.73.","graph":{"focus":["Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM"],"read":[{"series":"Run 1: 37 °C, 8.0 mM","x":20},{"series":"Run 2: 25 °C, 8.0 mM","x":20}]}},{"text":"The only difference between the runs is temperature, 37 °C vs 25 °C.","hl":"Run 1 and Run 2 began with 8.0 mM aspirin at 37 °C and 25 °C"},{"text":"In k = A e^(−Ea/RT), raising T raises the exponential term with Ea unchanged.","hl":"k = A e^(−Ea/RT)"}],"trap":"'Lowers the activation energy' describes a catalyst; Ea is treated as constant when the researchers use two temperatures to calculate it."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_chemphys_3","subj":"gen_chem","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Chem/Phys","title":"NADH Spectra and an LDH Assay","passage":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide carries electrons in metabolism in an oxidized form, NAD⁺, and a reduced form, NADH. Both forms contain an adenine ring that absorbs ultraviolet light strongly near 260 nm. Reduction of the nicotinamide ring, however, gives NADH a second absorption band centered near 340 nm, a difference that makes many dehydrogenase reactions easy to follow with a spectrophotometer.\n\nA student recorded the absorption spectra of 50.0 µM NAD⁺ and 50.0 µM NADH, each in pH 7.5 buffer in a cuvette with a 1.00 cm path length, measuring absorbance every 10 nm from 240 nm to 400 nm (Figure 1). Absorbance obeys the Beer–Lambert law, A = εbc, where ε is the molar absorptivity, b is the path length, and c is the molar concentration.\n\nThe student then used the spectra to design an assay for lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), which catalyzes Reaction 1.\n\nReaction 1: pyruvate + NADH + H⁺ → lactate + NAD⁺\n\nA 1.00 cm cuvette initially contained 2.0 mM pyruvate and 100 µM NADH. Two minutes after LDH was added, the absorbance at the monitoring wavelength had decreased by 0.31 from its starting value.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Absorption spectra of 50.0 µM NAD⁺ and 50.0 µM NADH, 1.00 cm path length","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":240,"max":400,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.1},"series":[{"name":"NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[240,0.4],[250,0.7],[260,0.9],[270,0.7],[280,0.32],[290,0.1],[300,0.03],[310,0.01],[320,0],[330,0],[340,0],[350,0],[360,0],[370,0],[380,0],[390,0],[400,0]]},{"name":"NADH, 50.0 µM","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[240,0.33],[250,0.57],[260,0.72],[270,0.57],[280,0.3],[290,0.12],[300,0.07],[310,0.12],[320,0.21],[330,0.28],[340,0.31],[350,0.29],[360,0.23],[370,0.15],[380,0.08],[390,0.03],[400,0.01]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Which wavelength should the student use to monitor the progress of Reaction 1?","choices":["260 nm, because absorbance is greatest there","280 nm, because proteins absorb there","340 nm, because only NADH absorbs there","400 nm, because neither form absorbs there"],"ans":2,"exp":"To follow NADH → NAD⁺, choose a wavelength where the two forms **differ most in absorbance**. At 340 nm NADH reads 0.31 and NAD⁺ reads 0.00, so any change in A reports NADH consumed.","how":{"ask":"Look for the wavelength where the reactant and product of the coenzyme half of the reaction can be told apart.","know":"• A good monitoring wavelength gives a large signal change as reactant becomes product.\n• If both forms absorb about equally, converting one to the other barely changes A.","steps":[{"text":"Read both at 260 nm: NAD⁺ 0.90, NADH 0.72 — both absorb strongly, so the change would be small.","graph":{"focus":["NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","x":260},{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":260}]}},{"text":"Read both at 340 nm: NADH 0.31, NAD⁺ 0.00.","graph":{"focus":["NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","x":340},{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":340}]}},{"text":"Only NADH absorbs at 340 nm, so A₃₄₀ tracks NADH alone.","hl":"gives NADH a second absorption band centered near 340 nm"}],"trap":"260 nm has the biggest absorbance, but both NAD⁺ and NADH absorb there, so converting one into the other changes A only slightly."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Based on Figure 1, the molar absorptivity of NADH at 340 nm is closest to:","choices":["1.6 × 10⁻⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹","6.2 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹","1.8 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹","3.1 × 10⁵ M⁻¹cm⁻¹"],"ans":1,"exp":"**ε = A/(bc)** = 0.31 ÷ (1.00 cm × 5.00 × 10⁻⁵ M) = 6.2 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹.","how":{"ask":"Use one plotted absorbance with the known concentration and path length.","know":"• Beer–Lambert: A = εbc, so ε = A/(bc).\n• Convert µM to M: 50.0 µM = 5.00 × 10⁻⁵ M.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● NADH at 340 nm: A = 0.31.","graph":{"focus":["NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":340}]}},{"text":"b = 1.00 cm, c = 50.0 µM = 5.00 × 10⁻⁵ M.","hl":"50.0 µM NADH, each in pH 7.5 buffer in a cuvette with a 1.00 cm path length"},{"text":"ε = 0.31 ÷ 5.00 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.2 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹.","hl":"A = εbc"}],"trap":"1.8 × 10⁴ uses the NAD⁺ reading at 260 nm, 0.90, the wrong curve and wrong wavelength; 1.6 × 10⁻⁴ is bc/A, the formula inverted."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"At 260 nm, the absorbance of the NAD⁺ solution is how many times that of the NADH solution?","choices":["0.80","1.25","1.8","2.9"],"ans":1,"exp":"Read both curves at 260 nm and divide **NAD⁺ by NADH**: 0.90 ÷ 0.72 = 1.25. Because concentrations and path length are equal, this is also the ratio of their ε values.","how":{"ask":"Read the two spectra at one wavelength and divide in the order the question names them.","know":"• 'A is how many times B' means A ÷ B.\n• With equal b and c, the absorbance ratio equals the ε ratio.","steps":[{"text":"Read ○ NAD⁺ at 260 nm: 0.90.","graph":{"focus":["NAD⁺, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","x":260}]}},{"text":"Read ● NADH at 260 nm: 0.72.","graph":{"focus":["NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":260}]}},{"text":"0.90 ÷ 0.72 = 1.25.","hl":"the absorbance of the NAD⁺ solution is how many times that of the NADH solution"}],"trap":"0.80 is 0.72 ÷ 0.90, the division done the wrong way; 2.9 divides NAD⁺ at 260 nm by NADH at 340 nm."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"How much lactate had LDH produced in the first two minutes of the assay?","choices":["25 µM","50 µM","100 µM","1.95 mM"],"ans":1,"exp":"ΔA₃₄₀ = 0.31 corresponds to Δ[NADH] = 0.31 ÷ (6.2 × 10³ × 1.00) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ M. **Reaction 1 is 1:1:1**, so 50 µM NADH consumed = 50 µM lactate made.","how":{"ask":"Turn the absorbance drop into moles of NADH used, then follow Reaction 1 to the product.","know":"• ΔA = ε b Δc at a wavelength where only one species absorbs.\n• Reaction 1 consumes 1 NADH and 1 pyruvate and makes 1 lactate and 1 NAD⁺.\n• Pyruvate, at 2.0 mM, is in excess and is not limiting.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● NADH at 340 nm: 0.31 for 50.0 µM, so a drop of 0.31 means 50 µM NADH was oxidized.","graph":{"focus":["NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":340}]}},{"text":"The assay's absorbance fell by exactly 0.31.","hl":"the absorbance at the monitoring wavelength had decreased by 0.31"},{"text":"Reaction 1: one NADH consumed per lactate formed → 50 µM lactate.","hl":"pyruvate + NADH + H⁺ → lactate + NAD⁺"}],"trap":"100 µM assumes all the NADH reacted; the starting A₃₄₀ for 100 µM would be 0.62, and only 0.31 of it disappeared. 1.95 mM is the pyruvate remaining."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"As Reaction 1 proceeds in the LDH assay, the absorbance at 260 nm would be expected to:","choices":["rise slightly","fall by the same amount as at 340 nm","stay exactly constant","fall to nearly zero"],"ans":0,"exp":"Each NADH becomes an NAD⁺. At 260 nm **NAD⁺ absorbs more than NADH** (0.90 vs 0.72 at 50 µM), so A₂₆₀ creeps up as the reaction runs.","how":{"ask":"Follow what each molecule of coenzyme turns into and compare their absorbances at 260 nm.","know":"• Total absorbance is the sum over species: A = b(ε₁c₁ + ε₂c₂).\n• Here the total coenzyme, NADH + NAD⁺, stays constant; only the mix changes.","steps":[{"text":"Every NADH consumed produces one NAD⁺.","hl":"lactate + NAD⁺"},{"text":"Read both at 260 nm: NADH 0.72, NAD⁺ 0.90 per 50 µM.","graph":{"focus":["NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":260},{"series":"NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","x":260}]}},{"text":"Converting 50 µM NADH to NAD⁺ changes A₂₆₀ by 0.90 − 0.72 = +0.18: a modest rise.","graph":{"focus":["NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","NADH, 50.0 µM"],"xLine":260}}],"trap":"'Fall by the same amount' assumes the 340 nm behavior applies at 260 nm, but the adenine band at 260 nm is present in both forms."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"In Reaction 1, which species is reduced?","choices":["Lactate","NADH","H⁺","Pyruvate"],"ans":3,"exp":"Pyruvate's ketone C=O becomes lactate's secondary alcohol C–OH: **gain of hydride, so pyruvate is reduced**. NADH donates the hydride and is oxidized to NAD⁺.","how":{"ask":"Decide which reactant gains electrons, or hydrogen, in the reaction.","know":"• Reduction = gain of electrons; in organic molecules, often gain of H or loss of bonds to O.\n• NADH is a reducing agent: it is oxidized to NAD⁺ as it reduces something else.","steps":[{"text":"Reaction 1 converts a ketone, pyruvate, into an alcohol, lactate.","hl":"pyruvate + NADH + H⁺ → lactate + NAD⁺"},{"text":"The coenzyme goes from reduced to oxidized form, losing its 340 nm band.","hl":"Reduction of the nicotinamide ring","graph":{"focus":["NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","NADH, 50.0 µM"],"read":[{"series":"NADH, 50.0 µM","x":340},{"series":"NAD⁺, 50.0 µM","x":340}]}},{"text":"So NADH is oxidized and pyruvate is the species reduced."}],"trap":"NADH is the reducing agent, so it is oxidized, not reduced; lactate is the product of reduction, not the species that undergoes it."}}]},
  {"id":"p_graph_chemphys_4","subj":"physics","topic":"Graph Reasoning · Chem/Phys","title":"Defibrillator Capacitor Discharge","passage":"An external defibrillator stores energy in a capacitor and then discharges it through the patient's chest to depolarize a critical mass of myocardium at once, terminating ventricular fibrillation. In a simple monophasic design, the capacitor discharges directly through the patient, whose transthoracic resistance R acts as the load. The voltage across the capacitor then decays exponentially, V(t) = V₀e^(−t/τ), where the time constant τ = RC is the time for the voltage to fall to about 37% of its initial value.\n\nEngineers tested a device whose capacitor has a capacitance C of 150 µF and is charged to an initial voltage V₀ of 2.0 kV. The capacitor was discharged through either a 50 Ω resistor, representing a patient with well-applied conductive gel pads, or a 100 Ω resistor, representing a patient with poor pad contact. Capacitor voltage was recorded with a high-voltage probe (Figure 1). The current through the load at any instant equals the capacitor voltage divided by the load resistance.\n\nThe energy stored in a capacitor is U = ½CV². Because the stored energy depends on the square of the voltage, the engineers noted that most of the stored energy leaves the capacitor well before the voltage approaches zero. Modern devices measure transthoracic impedance before a shock and adjust the waveform to compensate.","figure":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1  Capacitor voltage during discharge of a 150 µF defibrillator capacitor through two loads","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Capacitor voltage (kV)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.25},"series":[{"name":"50 Ω load","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2],[2.5,1.433],[5,1.027],[7.5,0.736],[10,0.527],[12.5,0.378],[15,0.271],[20,0.139],[25,0.071],[30,0.037]]},{"name":"100 Ω load","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2],[2.5,1.693],[5,1.433],[7.5,1.213],[10,1.027],[12.5,0.869],[15,0.736],[20,0.527],[25,0.378],[30,0.271]]}]},"questions":[{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"Based on Figure 1, the time constant for discharge through the 50 Ω load is closest to:","choices":["3.8 ms","5.2 ms","7.5 ms","15 ms"],"ans":2,"exp":"τ is the time for V to fall to **37% of V₀**: 0.37 × 2.0 kV = 0.74 kV. ● reaches 0.736 kV at 7.5 ms. Check: RC = 50 Ω × 150 µF = 7.5 ms.","how":{"ask":"Find the target voltage that defines τ, then find when the 50 Ω curve reaches it.","know":"• V(τ) = V₀e⁻¹ ≈ 0.37 V₀.\n• Half-life of the voltage is τ ln 2 ≈ 0.69τ, which is shorter than τ.","steps":[{"text":"37% of the starting voltage defines τ.","hl":"the time for the voltage to fall to about 37% of its initial value"},{"text":"Read ● at t = 0: 2.0 kV → target 0.74 kV.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"50 Ω load","x":0}],"yLine":0.74}},{"text":"Read ● at 7.5 ms: 0.736 kV → τ = 7.5 ms.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"50 Ω load","x":7.5}]}}],"trap":"5.2 ms is when the voltage reaches half of V₀, the half-life, not τ; 15 ms is where the 100 Ω curve, not the 50 Ω curve, reaches 0.74 kV."}},{"skill":"SIRS 2","q":"At t = 15 ms, the current through the 100 Ω load is how many times the current through the 50 Ω load?","choices":["0.37","0.73","1.4","2.7"],"ans":2,"exp":"The graph shows voltage, but **I = V/R**: 0.736 kV ÷ 100 Ω = 7.4 A and 0.271 kV ÷ 50 Ω = 5.4 A. 7.4 ÷ 5.4 ≈ 1.4.","how":{"ask":"The figure gives voltages; the question asks about currents.","know":"• Ohm's law: I = V/R, using the instantaneous capacitor voltage.\n• Convert kV to V before dividing by Ω.","steps":[{"text":"The y-axis is voltage; current must be computed.","hl":"The current through the load at any instant equals the capacitor voltage divided by the load resistance"},{"text":"Read both at 15 ms: ■ 100 Ω = 0.736 kV, ● 50 Ω = 0.271 kV.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load","100 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"100 Ω load","x":15},{"series":"50 Ω load","x":15}]}},{"text":"I₁₀₀ = 736 V ÷ 100 Ω = 7.4 A; I₅₀ = 271 V ÷ 50 Ω = 5.4 A; ratio = 7.4 ÷ 5.4 ≈ 1.4."}],"trap":"2.7 is the voltage ratio 0.736 ÷ 0.271, which forgets to divide each voltage by its own resistance; 0.73 is the current ratio inverted."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"How much energy is stored in the capacitor immediately before discharge?","choices":["0.15 J","300 J","600 J","1200 J"],"ans":1,"exp":"**U = ½CV²** = ½ × 150 × 10⁻⁶ F × (2.0 × 10³ V)² = 300 J, a typical maximum for an external defibrillator.","how":{"ask":"Apply the capacitor energy formula with SI units.","know":"• U = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C.\n• µF = 10⁻⁶ F; kV = 10³ V.","steps":[{"text":"Read either curve at t = 0: V₀ = 2.0 kV = 2.0 × 10³ V.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load","100 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"50 Ω load","x":0}]}},{"text":"C = 150 µF = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ F.","hl":"a capacitance C of 150 µF"},{"text":"U = ½ × 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ × 4.0 × 10⁶ = 300 J.","hl":"U = ½CV²"}],"trap":"600 J drops the ½; 0.15 J uses ½CV, forgetting to square the voltage."}},{"skill":"SIRS 4","q":"For the 50 Ω load, approximately how much energy has been delivered to the load during the first 7.5 ms?","choices":["40 J","110 J","190 J","260 J"],"ans":3,"exp":"At 7.5 ms ● reads 0.736 kV, so the capacitor still holds ½(150 µF)(736 V)² ≈ 41 J. **Energy delivered = 300 − 41 ≈ 260 J**, about 86%, because U scales with V².","how":{"ask":"Find what is left in the capacitor at 7.5 ms and subtract from what it started with.","know":"• Energy is conserved: energy leaving the capacitor is dissipated in the load.\n• U ∝ V², so at V = 0.37V₀, U = 0.37² U₀ ≈ 0.14 U₀.","steps":[{"text":"Read ● at 7.5 ms: 0.736 kV.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"50 Ω load","x":7.5}]}},{"text":"Remaining U = ½ × 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ × (736)² ≈ 41 J; initial U = 300 J.","hl":"Because the stored energy depends on the square of the voltage"},{"text":"Delivered = 300 − 41 ≈ 260 J.","hl":"most of the stored energy leaves the capacitor well before the voltage approaches zero"}],"trap":"190 J assumes energy falls in proportion to voltage, 300 × 0.37 = 110 J left; energy depends on V², so far more has been delivered. 40 J is what remains, not what was delivered."}},{"skill":"SIRS 3","q":"Which conclusion about doubling the load resistance from 50 Ω to 100 Ω is supported by Figure 1 and the passage?","choices":["It doubles τ and halves the initial current","It halves τ and doubles the initial current","It leaves τ unchanged and halves the energy stored","It doubles τ and doubles the energy stored"],"ans":0,"exp":"■ reaches 0.74 kV at 15 ms vs 7.5 ms for ●, so **τ = RC doubles**. Both start at 2.0 kV, so I₀ = V₀/R falls from 40 A to 20 A while the stored energy, ½CV₀², is unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Use the two curves to compare time constants, and the passage to compare starting current and stored energy.","know":"• τ = RC: τ ∝ R for a fixed capacitor.\n• I₀ = V₀/R.\n• Stored energy depends on C and V₀ only, not on the load.","steps":[{"text":"Read ■ at 15 ms: 0.736 kV = 37% of 2.0 kV → τ₁₀₀ = 15 ms; ● gave 7.5 ms.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load","100 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"100 Ω load","x":15},{"series":"50 Ω load","x":7.5}]}},{"text":"Both curves start at 2.0 kV: same V₀, so same stored energy.","graph":{"focus":["50 Ω load","100 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"100 Ω load","x":0}]}},{"text":"I₀ = 2000 V ÷ 50 Ω = 40 A vs 2000 V ÷ 100 Ω = 20 A.","hl":"the capacitor voltage divided by the load resistance"}],"trap":"Doubling the energy stored is wrong: the load does not change C or V₀, and both curves start at the same voltage. The higher-resistance curve is the slower one, so τ cannot be halved."}},{"skill":"SIRS 1","q":"Which modification would give a 50 Ω load the same time constant as the 100 Ω curve in Figure 1, without changing V₀?","choices":["Add an identical capacitor in series","Add an identical capacitor in parallel","Charge the capacitor to 4.0 kV","Replace the load with a 25 Ω resistor"],"ans":1,"exp":"The 100 Ω curve has τ = 15 ms; with R = 50 Ω this needs C = 300 µF. **Capacitors in parallel add**, so a second 150 µF in parallel doubles C and τ.","how":{"ask":"Work out what RC product the target curve has, then what change to C achieves it at 50 Ω.","know":"• Parallel capacitors: C_eq = C₁ + C₂.\n• Series capacitors: 1/C_eq = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂, so two equal ones give C/2.\n• τ = RC does not depend on V₀.","steps":[{"text":"Read ■ at 15 ms: 0.736 kV, so its τ = 15 ms.","graph":{"focus":["100 Ω load"],"read":[{"series":"100 Ω load","x":15}]}},{"text":"τ = RC → C = 15 ms ÷ 50 Ω = 300 µF, twice the original 150 µF.","hl":"τ = RC"},{"text":"Two 150 µF capacitors in parallel give 300 µF; in series they give only 75 µF."}],"trap":"A series connection halves C and so halves τ; charging to 4.0 kV changes the voltage scale but not τ, and it violates the condition on V₀."}}]},
];
